window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2020-09-1", "source": "2020年09月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the online library. You can start your essay with the sentence “The online library is becoming increasingly popular.” You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "The online library is becoming increasingly popular. More and more students and researchers now turn to\ndigital collections rather than walking to a physical reading room. This shift is not a passing trend but a\nreflection of how deeply the Internet has reshaped the way we learn.\nThere are two main reasons behind its growing appeal. For one thing, an online library allows us to access\nresources anytime and anywhere. Whether we are sitting at home or waiting for a bus, a single click brings a\nvast collection of e-books and journals to our screen. For another, it helps us search for information efficiently.\nInstead of flipping through hundreds of pages, we simply type in a keyword and locate exactly what we need\nwithin seconds, which saves an enormous amount of time.\nIn conclusion, the online library owes its popularity to its convenience and efficiency. As technology keeps\nadvancing, I firmly believe that it will play an even more important role in our future study and research."}, "translation": {"prompt": "茅台(Moutai)是中国最有名的白酒，在新中国成立前夕，被选为国宴用酒。 据说赤水沿岸的村民四千年前就开始酿造茅台。在西汉时期，那里的人们生产出了高质量的茅台，并把它贡给皇帝。自唐朝开始，这种地方酒通过海上丝绸之路运往海外。 茅台味道柔和，有一种特殊的香味；适量饮用可以帮助缓解疲劳，有镇静作用，因而广受国内外消费者的喜爱。", "sample": "Moutai is the most famous Chinese liquor, and on the eve of the founding of the People's Republic of China, it was selected as the official drink for state banquets. It is said that villagers along the Chishui River began to brew Moutai four thousand years ago. During the Western Han Dynasty, the local people produced high-quality Moutai and presented it to the emperor. Starting from the Tang Dynasty, this local liquor was shipped overseas via the Maritime Silk Road. Moutai has a mellow taste and a unique fragrance; when consumed in moderation, it can help relieve fatigue and has a calming effect, and thus it is very popular with consumers both at home and abroad."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2020-09-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "There are three main types of financial stress people encounter. The first type is apparent in people being stressed about the  __26__  ups and downs of investment markets—actually not so much the ups, but  __27__  the downs. These people are usually unable or unprepared to endure the long haul.\nThe next common type of financial stress is that caused by debt. In a  __28__  percentage of cases of debt-induced financial stress, credit cards and loans will be a central element. Often there will be a car loan and perhaps a mortgage, but credit cards often seem to be the gateway to debt-related financial difficulties for many.\nThe third type of stress and  __29__  the least known is inherited financial stress, which is the most destructive. It is experienced by those who have grown up in households where their parents regularly  __30__  and fought about money. Money therefore becomes a stressful topic, and so the thought of sitting down and planning is an unattractive  __31__  .\nThose suffering inherited financial anxiety  __32__  to follow one of two patterns. Either they put their head in the sand: they would  __33__  examining their financial statements, budgeting and discussing financial matters with those closest to them. Alternatively, they would go to the other  __34__  , and micro-analyze everything, to the point of complete  __35__  . They are convinced that whatever decision they make will be the wrong one.", "matchIntro": "Doctor's Orders: Let Children Just Play", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Imagine a drug that could enhance a child's creativity and critical thinking. Imagine that this drug were simple to make, safe to take, and could be had for free. The nation's leading pediatricians(儿科医生) say this miracle compound exists. In a new clinical report, they are urging doctors to prescribe it liberally to the children in their care."}, {"label": "B", "text": "\"This may seem old-fashioned, but there are skills to be learned when kids aren't told what to do,\" said Dr. Michael Yogman, a Harvard Medical School pediatrician who led the drafting of the call to arms. Whether it's rough physical play, outdoor play or social or pretend play, kids derive important lessons from the chance to make things up as they go, he said."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The advice, issued Monday by the American Academy of Pediatrics, may come as a shock to some parents. After spending years fretting(烦恼) over which toys to buy, which apps to download and which skill-building programs to send their kids to after school, letting them simply play—or better yet, playing with them—could seem like a step backward. The pediatricians insist that it's not. The academy's guidance does not include specific recommendations for the dosing of play. Instead, it asks doctors to advise parents before their babies turn two that play is essential to healthy development."}, {"label": "D", "text": "\"Play is not silly behavior,\" the academy's report declares. It fosters children's creativity, cooperation and problem-solving skills—all of which are critical for a 21st-century workforce. When parents engage in play with their children, it builds a wall against the harmful effects of all kinds of stress, including poverty, the academy says. In the pediatricians' view, essentially every life skill that's valued in adults can be built up with play. \"Collaboration, negotiation, decision-making, creativity, leadership, and increased physical activity are just some of the skills and benefits children gain through play,\" they wrote. The pediatricians' appeal comes as kids are being squeezed by increasing academic demands at school and the constant invasion of digital media."}, {"label": "E", "text": "The trends have been a long time coming. Between 1981 and 1997, detailed time-use studies showed that the time children spent at play declined by 25 percent. Since the adoption of sweeping education reforms in 2001, public schools have steadily increased the amount of time devoted to preparing for standardized tests. The focus on academic \"skills and drills\" has cut deeply into recess(课间休息) and other time for free play."}, {"label": "F", "text": "By 2009, a study of Los Angeles kindergarten classrooms found that five-year-olds were so burdened with academic requirements that they were down to an average of just 19 minutes per day of \"choice time,\" when they were permitted to play freely with blocks, toys or other children. One in four Los Angeles teachers reported there was no time at all for \"free play.\" Increased academic pressures have left 30 percent of U.S. kindergarten classes without any recess. Such findings prompted the American Academy of Pediatrics to issue a policy statement in 2013 on the \"crucial role of recess in school.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "Pediatricians aren't the only ones who have noticed. In a report titled \"Crisis in the Kindergarten,\" a group of educators, health professionals and child advocates called the loss of play in early childhood \"a tragedy, both for the children themselves and for our nation and world.\" Kids in play-based kindergartens \"end up equally good or better at reading and other intellectual skills, and they are more likely to become well-adjusted healthy people,\" the Alliance for Childhood said in 2009. Indeed, new research demonstrates why playing with blocks might have been time better spent, Yogman said. The trial assessed the effectiveness of an early mathematics intervention(干预) aimed at preschoolers. The results showed almost no gains in math achievement."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Another playtime thief: the growing proportion of kids' time spent in front of screens and digital devices, even among preschoolers. Last year, Common Sense Media reported that children up through age eight spent an average of two hours and 19 minutes in front of screens each day, including an average of 42 minutes a day for those under two. This increase of digital use comes with rising risks of obesity, sleep deprivation and cognitive(认知的), language and social-emotional delays, the American Academy of Pediatrics warned in 2016."}, {"label": "I", "text": "\"I respect that parents have busy lives and it's easy to hand a child an iPhone,\" Yogman said. \"But there's a cost to that. For young children, it's much too passive. And kids really learn better when they're actively engaged and have to really discover things.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "The decline of play is a special hazard for the roughly 1 in 5 children in the United States who live in poverty. These 14 million children most urgently need to develop the resilience(韧劲) that is cultivated with play. Instead, Yogman said, they are disproportionately affected by some of the trends that are making play scarce: academic pressures at schools that need to improve test scores, outside play areas that are limited or unsafe, and parents who lack the time or energy to share in playtime."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Yogman also worries about the pressures that squeeze playtime for more affluent kids. \"The notion that as parents we need to schedule every minute of their time is not doing them a great service,\" he said. Even well-meaning parents may be \"robbing them of the opportunity to have that joy of discovery and curiosity—the opportunity to find things out on their own.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "Play may not be a hard sell to kids. But UCLA pediatrician Carlos Lerner acknowledged that the pediatricians' new prescription may meet with skepticism(怀疑) from parents, who are anxious for advice on how to give their kids a leg up in the world. They should welcome the simplicity of the message, Lerner said. \"It's liberating to be able to offer them this advice: that your spending time with your child and letting him play is one of the most valuable things you can do,\" he said. \"It doesn't have to involve spending a lot of money or time, or joining a parenting group. It's something we can offer that's achievable. They just don't recognize it right now as particularly valuable.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Americans spend billions of dollars each year trying to change our weight with diets, gym memberships and plastic surgery.\nTrying to live up to the images of \"perfect\" models and movie heroes has a dark side: anxiety and depression, as well as unhealthy strategies for weight loss or muscle gain. It also has a financial cost. Having an eating disorder boosts annual health care costs by nearly US $2,000 per person.\nWhy is there both external and internal pressure to look \"perfect\"? One reason is that society rewards people who are thin and healthy looking. Researchers have shown that body mass index is related to wages and income. Especially for women, there is a clear penalty at work for being overweight or obese. Some studies have also found an impact for men, though a less noticeable one.\nWhile the research literature is clear that labor market success is partly based on how employers and customers perceive your body image, no one had explored the other side of the question. Does a person's own perception of body image matter to earnings and other indicators of success in the workplace?\nOur recently published study answered this question by tracking a large national random sample of Americans over a critical time period when bodies change from teenage shape into adult form and when people build their identities.\nAs in other research, women in our sample tend to over-perceive their weight—they think they're heavier than they are—while men tend to under-perceive theirs.\nWe found no relationship between the average person's self-perception of weight and labor market outcomes, although self-perceived weight can influence self-esteem(自尊心), mental health and health behaviors.\nWhile the continued gender penalty in the labor market is frustrating, our finding that misperceived weight does not harm workers is more heartening.\nSince employers' perception of weight is what matters in the labor market, changing discrimination laws to include body type as a category would also help. Michigan is the only state that prohibits discrimination on the basis of weight and height. We believe expanding such protections would make the labor market more fair and efficient.", "47": "Americans spend billions of dollars each year trying to change our weight with diets, gym memberships and plastic surgery.\nTrying to live up to the images of \"perfect\" models and movie heroes has a dark side: anxiety and depression, as well as unhealthy strategies for weight loss or muscle gain. It also has a financial cost. Having an eating disorder boosts annual health care costs by nearly US $2,000 per person.\nWhy is there both external and internal pressure to look \"perfect\"? One reason is that society rewards people who are thin and healthy looking. Researchers have shown that body mass index is related to wages and income. Especially for women, there is a clear penalty at work for being overweight or obese. Some studies have also found an impact for men, though a less noticeable one.\nWhile the research literature is clear that labor market success is partly based on how employers and customers perceive your body image, no one had explored the other side of the question. Does a person's own perception of body image matter to earnings and other indicators of success in the workplace?\nOur recently published study answered this question by tracking a large national random sample of Americans over a critical time period when bodies change from teenage shape into adult form and when people build their identities.\nAs in other research, women in our sample tend to over-perceive their weight—they think they're heavier than they are—while men tend to under-perceive theirs.\nWe found no relationship between the average person's self-perception of weight and labor market outcomes, although self-perceived weight can influence self-esteem(自尊心), mental health and health behaviors.\nWhile the continued gender penalty in the labor market is frustrating, our finding that misperceived weight does not harm workers is more heartening.\nSince employers' perception of weight is what matters in the labor market, changing discrimination laws to include body type as a category would also help. Michigan is the only state that prohibits discrimination on the basis of weight and height. We believe expanding such protections would make the labor market more fair and efficient.", "48": "Americans spend billions of dollars each year trying to change our weight with diets, gym memberships and plastic surgery.\nTrying to live up to the images of \"perfect\" models and movie heroes has a dark side: anxiety and depression, as well as unhealthy strategies for weight loss or muscle gain. It also has a financial cost. Having an eating disorder boosts annual health care costs by nearly US $2,000 per person.\nWhy is there both external and internal pressure to look \"perfect\"? One reason is that society rewards people who are thin and healthy looking. Researchers have shown that body mass index is related to wages and income. Especially for women, there is a clear penalty at work for being overweight or obese. Some studies have also found an impact for men, though a less noticeable one.\nWhile the research literature is clear that labor market success is partly based on how employers and customers perceive your body image, no one had explored the other side of the question. Does a person's own perception of body image matter to earnings and other indicators of success in the workplace?\nOur recently published study answered this question by tracking a large national random sample of Americans over a critical time period when bodies change from teenage shape into adult form and when people build their identities.\nAs in other research, women in our sample tend to over-perceive their weight—they think they're heavier than they are—while men tend to under-perceive theirs.\nWe found no relationship between the average person's self-perception of weight and labor market outcomes, although self-perceived weight can influence self-esteem(自尊心), mental health and health behaviors.\nWhile the continued gender penalty in the labor market is frustrating, our finding that misperceived weight does not harm workers is more heartening.\nSince employers' perception of weight is what matters in the labor market, changing discrimination laws to include body type as a category would also help. Michigan is the only state that prohibits discrimination on the basis of weight and height. We believe expanding such protections would make the labor market more fair and efficient.", "49": "Americans spend billions of dollars each year trying to change our weight with diets, gym memberships and plastic surgery.\nTrying to live up to the images of \"perfect\" models and movie heroes has a dark side: anxiety and depression, as well as unhealthy strategies for weight loss or muscle gain. It also has a financial cost. Having an eating disorder boosts annual health care costs by nearly US $2,000 per person.\nWhy is there both external and internal pressure to look \"perfect\"? One reason is that society rewards people who are thin and healthy looking. Researchers have shown that body mass index is related to wages and income. Especially for women, there is a clear penalty at work for being overweight or obese. Some studies have also found an impact for men, though a less noticeable one.\nWhile the research literature is clear that labor market success is partly based on how employers and customers perceive your body image, no one had explored the other side of the question. Does a person's own perception of body image matter to earnings and other indicators of success in the workplace?\nOur recently published study answered this question by tracking a large national random sample of Americans over a critical time period when bodies change from teenage shape into adult form and when people build their identities.\nAs in other research, women in our sample tend to over-perceive their weight—they think they're heavier than they are—while men tend to under-perceive theirs.\nWe found no relationship between the average person's self-perception of weight and labor market outcomes, although self-perceived weight can influence self-esteem(自尊心), mental health and health behaviors.\nWhile the continued gender penalty in the labor market is frustrating, our finding that misperceived weight does not harm workers is more heartening.\nSince employers' perception of weight is what matters in the labor market, changing discrimination laws to include body type as a category would also help. Michigan is the only state that prohibits discrimination on the basis of weight and height. We believe expanding such protections would make the labor market more fair and efficient.", "50": "Americans spend billions of dollars each year trying to change our weight with diets, gym memberships and plastic surgery.\nTrying to live up to the images of \"perfect\" models and movie heroes has a dark side: anxiety and depression, as well as unhealthy strategies for weight loss or muscle gain. It also has a financial cost. Having an eating disorder boosts annual health care costs by nearly US $2,000 per person.\nWhy is there both external and internal pressure to look \"perfect\"? One reason is that society rewards people who are thin and healthy looking. Researchers have shown that body mass index is related to wages and income. Especially for women, there is a clear penalty at work for being overweight or obese. Some studies have also found an impact for men, though a less noticeable one.\nWhile the research literature is clear that labor market success is partly based on how employers and customers perceive your body image, no one had explored the other side of the question. Does a person's own perception of body image matter to earnings and other indicators of success in the workplace?\nOur recently published study answered this question by tracking a large national random sample of Americans over a critical time period when bodies change from teenage shape into adult form and when people build their identities.\nAs in other research, women in our sample tend to over-perceive their weight—they think they're heavier than they are—while men tend to under-perceive theirs.\nWe found no relationship between the average person's self-perception of weight and labor market outcomes, although self-perceived weight can influence self-esteem(自尊心), mental health and health behaviors.\nWhile the continued gender penalty in the labor market is frustrating, our finding that misperceived weight does not harm workers is more heartening.\nSince employers' perception of weight is what matters in the labor market, changing discrimination laws to include body type as a category would also help. Michigan is the only state that prohibits discrimination on the basis of weight and height. We believe expanding such protections would make the labor market more fair and efficient.", "51": "The work-life balance is dead. By this, I'm not advocating that you should give up your pursuit of having a fulfilling career and a thriving personal life, and I'm definitely not saying that you have to give up one to have the other. I also acknowledge that we have a work-life problem, but I'm arguing that the concept of balance has never been helpful, because it's too limiting. You see, our language makes a difference, and how we refer to things matters because it affects our thinking and therefore our actions.\nAt the minimum, most of us work because we want to be able to support ourselves, our families, and the people around us. In the ideal world, we're all doing work that we're proud of and that provides meaning and purpose to us. But even if your job doesn't give you shivers of joy each new day, working is a part of what each of us does and the contribution we make to society. When you separate work and life, it's a little bit harder to make that connection. But when you think of work as part of a full life and a complete experience, it becomes easier to see that success in one aspect often supports another.\nLosing your balance and falling isn't pleasant. A goal to balance suggests that things could quickly get off balance, and that causes terrible outcomes. It's more constructive to think of solutions that continue to evolve over shifts in life and work. Rather than falling or failing, you may have good days or better days or not-so-good days. These variations are normal, and it's more useful to think of life as something that is ever evolving and changing, rather than a high-risk enterprise where things could go wrong with one misstep.\nHow we talk to ourselves matters, and how we talk about issues makes a difference. Let's bury \"work-life balance\" and think bigger and better about work-life fulfillment to do a little less balancing and a lot more living.", "52": "The work-life balance is dead. By this, I'm not advocating that you should give up your pursuit of having a fulfilling career and a thriving personal life, and I'm definitely not saying that you have to give up one to have the other. I also acknowledge that we have a work-life problem, but I'm arguing that the concept of balance has never been helpful, because it's too limiting. You see, our language makes a difference, and how we refer to things matters because it affects our thinking and therefore our actions.\nAt the minimum, most of us work because we want to be able to support ourselves, our families, and the people around us. In the ideal world, we're all doing work that we're proud of and that provides meaning and purpose to us. But even if your job doesn't give you shivers of joy each new day, working is a part of what each of us does and the contribution we make to society. When you separate work and life, it's a little bit harder to make that connection. But when you think of work as part of a full life and a complete experience, it becomes easier to see that success in one aspect often supports another.\nLosing your balance and falling isn't pleasant. A goal to balance suggests that things could quickly get off balance, and that causes terrible outcomes. It's more constructive to think of solutions that continue to evolve over shifts in life and work. Rather than falling or failing, you may have good days or better days or not-so-good days. These variations are normal, and it's more useful to think of life as something that is ever evolving and changing, rather than a high-risk enterprise where things could go wrong with one misstep.\nHow we talk to ourselves matters, and how we talk about issues makes a difference. Let's bury \"work-life balance\" and think bigger and better about work-life fulfillment to do a little less balancing and a lot more living.", "53": "The work-life balance is dead. By this, I'm not advocating that you should give up your pursuit of having a fulfilling career and a thriving personal life, and I'm definitely not saying that you have to give up one to have the other. I also acknowledge that we have a work-life problem, but I'm arguing that the concept of balance has never been helpful, because it's too limiting. You see, our language makes a difference, and how we refer to things matters because it affects our thinking and therefore our actions.\nAt the minimum, most of us work because we want to be able to support ourselves, our families, and the people around us. In the ideal world, we're all doing work that we're proud of and that provides meaning and purpose to us. But even if your job doesn't give you shivers of joy each new day, working is a part of what each of us does and the contribution we make to society. When you separate work and life, it's a little bit harder to make that connection. But when you think of work as part of a full life and a complete experience, it becomes easier to see that success in one aspect often supports another.\nLosing your balance and falling isn't pleasant. A goal to balance suggests that things could quickly get off balance, and that causes terrible outcomes. It's more constructive to think of solutions that continue to evolve over shifts in life and work. Rather than falling or failing, you may have good days or better days or not-so-good days. These variations are normal, and it's more useful to think of life as something that is ever evolving and changing, rather than a high-risk enterprise where things could go wrong with one misstep.\nHow we talk to ourselves matters, and how we talk about issues makes a difference. Let's bury \"work-life balance\" and think bigger and better about work-life fulfillment to do a little less balancing and a lot more living.", "54": "The work-life balance is dead. By this, I'm not advocating that you should give up your pursuit of having a fulfilling career and a thriving personal life, and I'm definitely not saying that you have to give up one to have the other. I also acknowledge that we have a work-life problem, but I'm arguing that the concept of balance has never been helpful, because it's too limiting. You see, our language makes a difference, and how we refer to things matters because it affects our thinking and therefore our actions.\nAt the minimum, most of us work because we want to be able to support ourselves, our families, and the people around us. In the ideal world, we're all doing work that we're proud of and that provides meaning and purpose to us. But even if your job doesn't give you shivers of joy each new day, working is a part of what each of us does and the contribution we make to society. When you separate work and life, it's a little bit harder to make that connection. But when you think of work as part of a full life and a complete experience, it becomes easier to see that success in one aspect often supports another.\nLosing your balance and falling isn't pleasant. A goal to balance suggests that things could quickly get off balance, and that causes terrible outcomes. It's more constructive to think of solutions that continue to evolve over shifts in life and work. Rather than falling or failing, you may have good days or better days or not-so-good days. These variations are normal, and it's more useful to think of life as something that is ever evolving and changing, rather than a high-risk enterprise where things could go wrong with one misstep.\nHow we talk to ourselves matters, and how we talk about issues makes a difference. Let's bury \"work-life balance\" and think bigger and better about work-life fulfillment to do a little less balancing and a lot more living.", "55": "The work-life balance is dead. By this, I'm not advocating that you should give up your pursuit of having a fulfilling career and a thriving personal life, and I'm definitely not saying that you have to give up one to have the other. I also acknowledge that we have a work-life problem, but I'm arguing that the concept of balance has never been helpful, because it's too limiting. You see, our language makes a difference, and how we refer to things matters because it affects our thinking and therefore our actions.\nAt the minimum, most of us work because we want to be able to support ourselves, our families, and the people around us. In the ideal world, we're all doing work that we're proud of and that provides meaning and purpose to us. But even if your job doesn't give you shivers of joy each new day, working is a part of what each of us does and the contribution we make to society. When you separate work and life, it's a little bit harder to make that connection. But when you think of work as part of a full life and a complete experience, it becomes easier to see that success in one aspect often supports another.\nLosing your balance and falling isn't pleasant. A goal to balance suggests that things could quickly get off balance, and that causes terrible outcomes. It's more constructive to think of solutions that continue to evolve over shifts in life and work. Rather than falling or failing, you may have good days or better days or not-so-good days. These variations are normal, and it's more useful to think of life as something that is ever evolving and changing, rather than a high-risk enterprise where things could go wrong with one misstep.\nHow we talk to ourselves matters, and how we talk about issues makes a difference. Let's bury \"work-life balance\" and think bigger and better about work-life fulfillment to do a little less balancing and a lot more living."}, "questions": [{"id": "2020-09-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What has caused the thick clouds in the photos taken by NASA satellite?", "options": ["A) Ship traffic in the Atlantic.", "B) Warm currents in the ocean.", "C) Exhaust from cars in Europe.", "D) Particles emitted by power plants."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Ship traffic in the Atlantic.\n【定位】NASA officials explained those thicker clouds are signs of ship traffic below. When ships\npower their way through the ocean, they pump exhaust into the atmosphere, just as cars do.\nAnd those massive amounts of particles can cause clouds to form.\nNASA 官员解释说，那些更厚的云是下方船只航行的标志。当船只破浪穿行于海洋时，它们\n会像汽车一样把尾气排入大气。而那大量的颗粒物能够促使云的形成。\n【信号】因果词 cause 连起两端。答案信号是因果动词：those massive amounts of particles can\ncause clouds to form 里的 cause 直接把'船排出的颗粒物'与'云的形成'连起来，再往前\nthose thicker clouds are signs of ship traffic below 点明云来自船，因果链清楚。\n【替换】those thicker clouds are signs of ship traffic below ↔ Ship traffic in the Atlantic.\n原文 ship traffic 与选项同源\nthose massive amounts of particles can cause clouds to form ↔ Ship traffic in the Atlantic.\n颗粒来自船故归因于船\n【排除】B) 未提及\nWarm currents in the ocean. 听着像海洋话题，但录音通篇没说暖流造成云；只提到\nNorth Atlantic 这片海域，未提 warm currents，属凭海洋联想的无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\nExhaust from cars in Europe. 偷了录音里的原词：录音确实说船排尾气 just as cars do，\n只是拿汽车作类比，真正造云的是船不是汽车；选项把类比对象 cars 当成了真凶，是典型\n原词陷阱。\n借用原文：they pump exhaust into the atmosphere, just as cars do\nD) 原词陷阱\nParticles emitted by power plants. 盗了录音原词 particles：录音的颗粒物是船排出的\n(ships…pump exhaust)，而非 power plants 电厂；录音从头到尾没出现 power plants，\n选项把颗粒物的来源偷换成了电厂。\n借用原文：they pump exhaust into the atmosphere", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What do NASA officials think about the thick clouds?", "options": ["A) They need to be taken seriously.", "B) They have a huge effect on fishery.", "C) They might be causing trouble to air flights.", "D) They may be affecting the world's climate."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） They may be affecting the world's climate.\n【定位】It's likely that these sorts of clouds are having some effect on the global climate, according to\nNASA officials. But scientists aren't yet sure what effect it has.\n据 NASA 官员说，这类云很可能正对全球气候产生某种影响。但科学家尚不确定它具体有\n什么影响。\n【信号】结尾结论句 + according to。答案信号是录音结尾的下结论措辞：It's likely that these\nsorts of clouds are having some effect on the global climate, according to NASA\nofficials，It's likely 表推测性判断、according to NASA officials 点明这正是官员的看法，\n态度题答案常落在这种结尾结论句。\n【替换】these sorts of clouds are having some effect on the global climate ↔\nThey may be affecting the world's climate.\nhaving effect on=affecting 同义\nIt's likely that ↔ They may be affecting the world's climate.\nIt's likely=may 同度推测\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey need to be taken seriously. 听起来像合理表态，但录音里官员只说'很可能影响气\n候'，从未说要'认真对待/重视'这些云，属于没有依据的拔高，原文未提。\nB) 原词陷阱\nThey have a huge effect on fishery. 偷了录音里的原词：录音前面说云 These clouds\ncan be huge，又说 having some effect on the global climate，选项拼凑 huge 和\neffect，却把对象换成了根本没提的 fishery 渔业，是原词陷阱叠加无中生有。\n借用原文：These clouds can be huge\nC) 偷换概念\nThey might be causing trouble to air flights. 借了录音'有某种影响'的意思，却把受影响\n对象换成 air flights 航班——录音只说影响 global climate，从未提航班，属于把影响对象\n偷换。\n借用原文：having some effect on the global climate\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Staff at a suburban supermarket in Melbourne say they feel unsafe at work after security guards were\nremoved. This came after a series of physical attacks and verbal abuse by customers. More than 50\nworkers at the store have signed a letter calling for a permanent security guard following a series of\nincidents, including a customer threatening to attack a supervisor with a knife. A security worker had\nguarded the store each night from 7 p.m. until 12 a.m., but that had stopped suddenly on Monday,\nemployees said. One worker said an angry customer had thrown a chicken at his head after complaining\nabout how long she had waited to be served. Another worker said the lack of protection at the store\nmade her feel uncomfortable at work. However, the spokesman of the supermarket said the store had\ntaken strong action in response to incidents. \"We have found very few instances of bad customer\nbehavior at our store in the past year. In the rare cases we have seen bad behavior, we have taken strong\naction in response, including banning a customer from the store.\"", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "For what purpose did the staff at a supermarket in Melbourne sign a letter?", "options": ["A) To appeal for higher wages.", "B) To demand better health care.", "C) To call for a permanent security guard.", "D) To dismiss the bad-tempered supervisor."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） To call for a permanent security guard.\n【定位】More than 50 workers at the store have signed a letter calling for a permanent security guard\nfollowing a series of incidents, including a customer threatening to attack a supervisor with\na knife.\n店里 50 多名员工签署了一封信，在一连串事件（包括一名顾客威胁要持刀袭击一位主管）\n之后，呼吁配备一名长期保安。\n【信号】动词 signed a letter 紧跟诉求 calling for。答案句的信号是\"做该动作的动词 + 紧跟的诉\n求短语\"：原文 have signed a letter calling for a permanent security guard 里，signed\na letter 正是题干问的\"签信\"动作，其后 calling for（呼吁）后面的名词就是签信的目的所\n在，顺势锁定这句即可。\n【替换】calling for a permanent security guard ↔ To call for a permanent security guard.\n现在分词转不定式，措辞几乎一致\n【排除】A) 未提及\nTo appeal for higher wages. 说\"为加薪\"。录音通篇没提工资，只谈安全与保安——这\n是\"员工签信常被想成为了待遇\"的常识替代，属未提及。\nB) 偷换概念\nTo demand better health care. 说\"为更好的医疗\"。录音从未提 health care，谈的全是人\n身安全（security / guard）。它把\"安全\"偷换成\"医疗保健\"，靠 care 一类词制造\"好像听\n过\"的熟悉感。\nD) 原词陷阱\nTo dismiss the bad-tempered supervisor. 说\"为开除坏脾气的主管\"。supervisor 确实是\n录音原词，但原文是 a customer threatening to attack a supervisor with a knife——主\n管是\"被顾客威胁\"的受害者，不是员工要开除的对象。它盗用 supervisor 让你\"听到就想\n选\"，实则张冠李戴。\n借用原文：a customer threatening to attack a supervisor with a knife", "points": ["听力·长对话", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What did the spokesman of the supermarket say regarding the employees' demand?", "options": ["A) It had already taken strong action.", "B) It would put customers' needs first.", "C) It would take their appeal seriously.", "D) It was seeking help from the police."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It had already taken strong action.\n【定位】However, the spokesman of the supermarket said the store had taken strong action in response\nto incidents.\n然而，超市的发言人说，门店已经针对这些事件采取了有力的应对行动。\n【信号】转折 However + 引述 spokesman said。双重信号锁定这句：一是 However（然而）这\n个转折，前面讲的是员工的不满与诉求，however 之后才轮到题干问的\"发言人\"开口；二是\n引述词 the spokesman of the supermarket said，它后面紧跟的就是发言人原话。两信号\n叠加，答案句无疑。\n【替换】had taken strong action in response to incidents ↔ It had already taken strong action.\n原话保留 + 加 already 点出已完成\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt would put customers' needs first. 说\"会把顾客需求放首位\"。发言人确实提到顾客，但\n从没说\"以顾客为先\"；这是借\"超市当然重视顾客\"的常识硬填，属未提及。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt would take their appeal seriously. 说\"会认真对待诉求\"，是最像标准回应、最顺耳的一\n项。但发言人原话是 had taken strong action——讲的是\"已经做了有力行动\"，不是\"将会\n认真对待\"。它把\"已做\"偷换成\"将认真对待\"，且这话录音根本没说。\nD) 未提及\nIt was seeking help from the police. 说\"正在向警方求助\"。录音里发言人提到的应对是\nbanning a customer from the store（封禁顾客），从未提报警 / police。它顺着\"安全事\n件常报警\"的惯性硬接，属未提及。\n借用原文：banning a customer from the store", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What did drivers on the motorway to Warsaw find?", "options": ["A) The road was flooded.", "B) The road was blocked.", "C) The road was frozen with snow.", "D) The road was covered with spilled gas."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The road was blocked.\n【定位】Drivers on their way to the Polish capital of Warsaw on Wednesday morning found the road\nblocked by an unusual obstacle: tons of liquid chocolate that spilled onto the motorway.\n周三早晨，前往波兰首都华沙的司机们发现道路被一个不寻常的障碍物堵住了：数吨洒到高\n速公路上的液态巧克力。\n【信号】题干原话 found 直接对上。新闻开篇第一句就出现 Drivers on their way to the Polish\ncapital of Warsaw on Wednesday morning found the road blocked，和题干 What\ndid drivers on the motorway to Warsaw find? 用同一个动词 found、同一批主语\ndrivers——题干词在录音里复现，就是答案句的最强信号。\n【替换】found the road blocked ↔ The road was blocked.\n几乎原句，主被动微调\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说 liquid chocolate that spilled onto the motorway（液态巧克力洒到路上），选项\nThe road was flooded. 把\"洒出液体\"夸成\"被水淹\"。听到 liquid、spilled 就想到\"淹\"，但\n录音的状态词是 blocked（堵），不是 flooded（淹）。\n借用原文：tons of liquid chocolate that spilled onto the motorway\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音后面消防员说清理巧克力 was worse than dealing with snow（比处理雪还难），只\n是拿\"雪\"作比较；选项 The road was frozen with snow. 却把\"雪\"说成路面真被雪冻住。\nsnow 是录音念过的词，但它出现在比喻里，不是路的实际状况。\n借用原文：removing the chocolate was worse than dealing with snow\nD) 偷换概念\n录音洒的是 liquid chocolate（液态巧克力），选项 The road was covered with spilled\ngas. 把\"洒出物\"偷换成 gas（汽油）。\"路被洒出物覆盖\"这个框架对，但具体物质被换错\n了。\n借用原文：tons of liquid chocolate that spilled onto the motorway", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the report say about the accident?", "options": ["A) A truck plunged into a pool of liquid chocolate.", "B) The heavy snow made driving very difficult.", "C) The truck driver dozed off while driving.", "D) A truck hit a barrier and overturned."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） A truck hit a barrier and overturned.\n【定位】A truck carrying the sweet load hit a road barrier and overturned, blocking two lanes.\n一辆载着甜蜜货物的卡车撞上路边护栏后翻车，堵住了两条车道。\n【信号】动作链 hit…and overturned 交代经过。这句 A truck carrying the sweet load hit a\nroad barrier and overturned 用\"撞护栏→翻车\"的动作链直接讲清事故怎么发生，是题干\n问 about the accident 的正解所在；新闻顺听到第二句即落点。\n【替换】hit a road barrier and overturned ↔ A truck hit a barrier and overturned.\n近乎原词，仅省 road\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项 A truck plunged into a pool of liquid chocolate. 说卡车\"冲进巧克力液池\"，把巧克\n力当成事故起因；但录音是先 hit a road barrier and overturned、罐裂后才 spilled a\npool of rapidly-hardening chocolate——巧克力是翻车的结果，不是起因。pool、liquid\nchocolate 都是录音原词，顺序却被颠倒。\n借用原文：The cracked tank spilled a pool of rapidly-hardening chocolate\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 The heavy snow made driving very difficult. 把事故归因于大雪，但录音里 snow\n只出现在 was worse than dealing with snow 的比喻中，根本没说当天下雪、更没说雪致\n驾驶困难。这是借\"念过的 snow\"硬造一个录音没有的起因。\n借用原文：removing the chocolate was worse than dealing with snow\nC) 未提及\n选项 The truck driver dozed off while driving. 说司机开车打瞌睡，录音只提到 the\ndriver was taken to hospital with a broken arm（司机手臂骨折送医），从没说他瞌\n睡。\"卡车出事=司机疲劳\"是常识脑补，录音并未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What did the firefighters' representative tell the local TV?", "options": ["A) It was a long time before the cleanup was finished.", "B) It was a hard task to remove the spilled substance.", "C) It was fortunate that no passenger got injured.", "D) It was difficult to contact the manufacturer."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It was a hard task to remove the spilled substance.\n【定位】A representative for the firefighters told the local TV that removing the chocolate was worse\nthan dealing with snow.\n消防部门的一位发言人告诉当地电视台，清除这些巧克力比处理积雪还要麻烦。\n【信号】told 引出的直接引语。录音 A representative for the firefighters told the local TV that\nremoving the chocolate was worse than dealing with snow 用 told the local TV 引出\n发言人原话，与题干 What did the firefighters' representative tell the local TV? 的 tell\nthe local TV 一字对应——\"told\"就是答案句的信号词。\n【替换】removing the chocolate was worse than dealing with snow ↔\nIt was a hard task to remove the spilled substance.\n\"比雪还糟\"=\"很难\"，巧克力=洒出物\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n选项 It was a long time before the cleanup was finished. 说清理\"花了很久\"。录音里确\n有 the long task ahead 暗示任务漫长，但那是叙述者的话、不是发言人 told 的内容；发\n言人说的是\"难\"（worse than snow），不是\"久\"。盗用了 long 一词，又张冠李戴换了说\n话人。\n借用原文：cheerful about the long task ahead\nC) 未提及\n选项 It was fortunate that no passenger got injured. 说幸好没乘客受伤，但录音明说\nthe driver was taken to hospital with a broken arm——司机手臂骨折，且全程没提乘\n客。既与\"有人受伤\"相悖，也不是发言人说的内容。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 It was difficult to contact the manufacturer. 说难联系厂家。录音确有 After\ncontacting the chocolate manufacturer（联系了厂家之后），说明厂家已联系上、根本\n不难；contact、manufacturer 都是原词，却凭空加了\"困难\"，且非发言人 told 的内容。\n借用原文：After contacting the chocolate manufacturer, the firefighters resorted to spraying hot,\npressurized water", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "Why did the woman decide to go back to cash for payment?", "options": ["A) She wanted to save for a new phone.", "B) She found it much safer to use cash.", "C) She could enjoy discount with cash.", "D) She had been cheated using phone apps."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She wanted to save for a new phone.\n【定位】I'm trying to save money for a new phone, and I find that using cash rather than payment apps\nhelps me to save.\n我正想攒钱买部新手机，而且我发现用现金而不用支付 App 能帮我省钱。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答。男方一开口就问她为何用现金，女方紧接着应答 I'm trying to save\nmoney for a new phone——这是被问者对\"为什么\"的直接交代，问题落在回答方，这一\n句就是答案句。\n【替换】save money for a new phone ↔ save for a new phone\n近乎同义·略去 money\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 借用了录音反复出现的 using cash 这个原词，但她说用现金是为了\"省钱\"，全程没说现\n金\"更安全（safer）\"，B 把\"省钱\"偷换成了\"安全\"，听到 cash 就选会中招。\n借用原文：using cash rather than payment apps\nC) 未提及\nC 说用现金能\"享受折扣（discount）\"，但录音里女方只讲省钱与心理感受，从未提到现金\n有任何折扣，属凭空添加的理由。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 借用录音里的 using an app / phone apps 这一原词，编出\"用 App 被骗过\n（cheated）\"的理由，但录音从头到尾没提任何\"被骗\"的事，是用熟词加吓人情节做的干\n扰。\n借用原文：transfer from your bank account to the seller's bank account using an app", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What happens when people use apps for payment according to the woman?", "options": ["A) They can save a lot more time and trouble.", "B) They find it less difficult to make purchases.", "C) They derive greater pleasure from buying things.", "D) They are less aware of the value of their money."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are less aware of the value of their money.\n【定位】I believe we have less connection with the value of our money when we just tap the\n\"Approve\" buttons on our phones.\n我觉得，当我们只是在手机上点一下\"确认\"键时，我们和钱的价值之间的联系就变弱了。\n【信号】观点句 I believe 引出结论。女方用 I believe we have less connection with the value of\nour money 给出明确判断，I believe 是她下结论的信号词，加上题干限定 according to\nthe woman，这句正是答案句。\n【替换】we have less connection with the value of our money ↔\nThey are less aware of the value of their money.\n同义替换·connection 换成 aware\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 说用 App\"省时省力（save time and trouble）\"，听上去是手机支付的常识好处，但女\n方这段讲的是\"对钱的价值感变弱\"，根本没说省时省事，A 是用生活常识脑补、答非所问。\nB) 答非所问\nB 说用 App 让人\"更容易买东西（less difficult to make purchases）\"，与女方强调的\"对\n钱的价值更没感觉\"不是一回事，是另一个看似合理却未被女方说出的方向。\nC) 未提及\nC 说人们\"从买东西中获得更多快乐（greater pleasure）\"，但录音里女方谈的是花钱时的\n心理\"麻木\"，从未说更快乐，pleasure 是凭空加进来的情绪词。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What might the man tend to buy with payment apps?", "options": ["A) More valuable items.", "B) More non-essential things.", "C) Everyday necessities.", "D) Electronic devices."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） More non-essential things.\n【定位】Since I stopped carrying cash around and started using my phone apps to pay, I may have\ndeveloped a tendency to buy more small or non-essential items.\n自从我不再随身带现金、开始用手机 App 付款以来，我可能养成了买更多小件或非必需品\n的倾向。\n【信号】被问者本人自述 tendency to buy。题目问\"the man\"买什么，答案就在男方自陈 a\ntendency to buy more small or non-essential items 这句——这是当事人对自己购买倾\n向的直接陈述，tendency to buy 是定位信号。\n【替换】buy more small or non-essential items ↔ More non-essential things.\n近乎原义·items 换 things\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 说买\"更贵重的东西（valuable items）\"，借用了录音 items 这个原词，但男方说的是\n买\"small（小件）\"非必需品，与\"贵重\"正好相反，极性反了。\n借用原文：buy more small or non-essential items\nC) 与原文相反\nC 的\"everyday necessities（日常必需品）\"与录音 non-essential items 只差一个否定前\n缀，盗用了\"essential\"这个词根，却把\"非必需\"偷换成\"必需\"，意思正好掉头。\n借用原文：non-essential items\nD) 未提及\nD 说买\"电子产品（electronic devices）\"，因为整段在聊\"手机、App\"显得很搭，但录音从\n没说男方买电子产品，是借话题氛围编出来的干扰。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the man think of electronic payment?", "options": ["A) It can improve shopping efficiency.", "B) It is altering the way of shopping.", "C) It may lead to excessive spending.", "D) It appeals more to younger people."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） It may lead to excessive spending.\n【定位】I stopped using my credit card because I found I was spending excessively. Perhaps I should\ntake the same approach of paying using my phone.\n我已经不用信用卡了，因为我发现自己花钱太过头。也许我对手机付款也该采取同样的做法\n（不用它）。\n【信号】负面措辞 spending excessively 透态度。男方说 I found I was spending excessively 并\n打算对手机付款\"take the same approach\"（即也要戒掉），spending excessively 这个\n负面措辞，加上他把电子支付与刷爆的信用卡相提并论，暴露了他认为电子支付会让人\"过\n度消费\"的态度。\n【替换】I found I was spending excessively ↔ It may lead to excessive spending.\n同根改写·动词转名词\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 说电子支付\"提高购物效率（improve shopping efficiency）\"，是手机支付的常识优\n点，但男方全程在讲它让人乱花钱，从没夸它效率高，A 靠常识脑补、与他的负面态度不\n符。\nB) 答非所问\nB 说电子支付\"正在改变购物方式（altering the way of shopping）\"，这话本身没错、也\n算贴题，但它只是客观描述\"在变\"，没表明男方\"怎么看\"，题问的是态度（think of），B 答\n非所问。\nD) 未提及\nD 说电子支付\"更吸引年轻人（appeals more to younger people）\"，但录音里两人从没\n谈到年龄或年轻人，younger people 是凭空添加的内容。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Why did the man call the woman?", "options": ["A) He wanted to order some wooden furniture.", "B) He had to change the furniture delivery time.", "C) He had a problem with the furniture delivered.", "D) He wanted the furniture store to give him a refund."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） He had a problem with the furniture delivered.\n【定位】M: No, no. The table is fine. The problem is the chairs.\n男：不不，桌子没问题。问题出在椅子上。\n【信号】男方应答点出问题所在。女方先问是不是桌子出了问题，男方应答 The problem is the\nchairs.，这是被问者的直接应答、也是他亮明来意的落点——他打电话正是因为送来的椅子\n有问题，应答句里的 problem 就是答案信号。\n【替换】The problem is the chairs. ↔ He had a problem with the furniture delivered.\nchairs 上位概括为 furniture\nthe delivery was this morning ↔ the furniture delivered\ndelivery 名词转 delivered 分词\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音开头女方提到 the wooden table，后文又有 purchased、order 等词，A 项 He\nwanted to order some wooden furniture 把 wooden、order 这些原词拼起来，但男方\n打电话不是去下新订单，而是投诉送错货，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：the wooden table\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确实多次出现 delivery、this morning、tomorrow 9 a.m. 等时间与送货词，B 项 He\nhad to change the furniture delivery time 借这些词制造假象，但男方从没要求更改送货\n时间，改时间的话是女方为退换货才提的。\n借用原文：the delivery was this morning\nD) 未提及\n买家投诉容易让人联想到要退款，但录音里男方只要求换成对的椅子，女方也只承诺换货，\n自始至终没人提 refund，D 项 He wanted the furniture store to give him a refund 是凭\n常识脑补、录音未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What did the woman ask the man to do?", "options": ["A) Send the furniture back to the store.", "B) Describe the furniture he received.", "C) Collect the furniture he ordered.", "D) Buy another brand of furniture."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Describe the furniture he received.\n【定位】W: Do you mind describing the chairs that were delivered to your apartment, Mr. Brown?\n女：布朗先生，您介意描述一下送到您公寓的那些椅子吗？\n【信号】Do you mind 是请求信号。女方说 Do you mind describing the chairs，Do you mind\n加动名词是典型的礼貌请求句式，这正是她请男方做某事的那一句，请求的动作就是\ndescribing，是本题答案信号。\n【替换】describing the chairs that were delivered ↔ Describe the furniture he received\ndescribe 换形且 chairs 概括为 furniture\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音后文女方说 We will send a van to collect those four，是商家派车来取、把货送回店\n里，A 项 Send the furniture back to the store 把这个送回动作安到男方头上，可这是商\n家做的、不是女方请男方做的，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：We will send a van to collect those four\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里 collect 出自女方的 send a van to collect those four，是商家来取货，C 项\nCollect the furniture he ordered 借 collect 一词制造假象，但取货是商家的事，且\ncollect 的是错送的椅子不是他订的货，方向也反了。\n借用原文：send a van to collect those four\nD) 未提及\nD 项 Buy another brand of furniture 说让男方另买一个牌子的家具，录音里女方只承诺换\n成原来订的 Milano 椅子，从没让他改买别的品牌，brand 一词也未出现，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What did the woman promise to do for the man?", "options": ["A) Correct their mistake.", "B) Improve their service.", "C) Apologize to his wife.", "D) Give the money back."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Correct their mistake.\n【定位】W: ... I'm terribly sorry. We will send a van to collect those four and replace them with the\nMilano you purchased. Will tomorrow 9 a.m. be okay, Mr. Brown?\n女：……非常抱歉。我们会派一辆车来把这四把收走，换成您当初买的 Milano 椅子。明天\n上午九点方便吗，布朗先生？\n【信号】We will 是承诺信号。女方用将来时 We will send a van to collect those four and\nreplace them with the Milano you purchased，We will 表明这是商家做出的承诺，承诺\n的核心动作是收走错货、换成对的，正是本题答案信号。\n【替换】replace them with the Milano you purchased ↔ Correct their mistake\n具体换货概括为纠正错误\nthere must have been some confusion with the order ↔ mistake\nconfusion 同义于 mistake\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB 项 Improve their service 说商家承诺改进服务，但录音里女方只承诺这一单的换货处\n理，从没说今后要改善服务，service 一词也没出现，属凭印象拔高、未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里女方确实说了 I'm terribly sorry，C 项 Apologize to his wife 借这个道歉做文章，\n但她是向男方布朗先生道歉、不是向他妻子，且本题问的是承诺去做的事，道歉是当场动作\n不是承诺，方向也错。\n借用原文：I'm terribly sorry\nD) 未提及\nD 项 Give the money back 退钱，是顾客投诉时最容易联想到的结果，但录音里女方的承\n诺是换货不是退款，全程没出现 refund 或 money back，属凭常识想当然、未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What did the woman do at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) She recommended a new style.", "B) She offered some gift to the man.", "C) She apologized to the man once more.", "D) She checked all the items with the man."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She apologized to the man once more.\n【定位】W: Okay, great. Once again, I'm sorry for the confusion and trouble caused.\n女：好的。再一次，对于这次弄错和造成的麻烦我很抱歉。\n【信号】末句 Once again sorry 是收尾道歉。录音最后一句女方说 Once again, I'm sorry for the\nconfusion and trouble caused，Once again 加 sorry 是对话结尾再次致歉的明确信号，\n正对应题干的 at the end，是本题答案句。\n【替换】Once again, I'm sorry ↔ She apologized to the man once more\nbe sorry 转 apologize、once again 转 once more\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 She recommended a new style 说女方推荐新款式，但录音里她只承诺换回男方原订\n的 Milano，从没推荐任何新样式，style、recommend 都没出现，属未提及。\nB) 未提及\nB 项 She offered some gift to the man 说女方送礼，录音里女方只做了道歉和安排换\n货，从未提供任何礼物，gift 一词全程没出现，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音前文男方自己核对过 The mirror and two paintings are here 等物品，D 项 She\nchecked all the items with the man 借这个核对场景做文章，但核对清单的是男方、且发\n生在对话中段不是结尾，本题问的是女方在结尾做的事，人和时点都对不上。\n借用原文：The mirror and two paintings are here", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What is Marie Kondo's TV show about?", "options": ["A) Reading books of wisdom.", "B) Tidying up one's home.", "C) Sharing with others.", "D) Donating to charity."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） Tidying up one's home.\n【定位】Say hello to a TV show called \"Tidying Up with Marie Kondo\", a home improvement show\nbased on her wildly popular book The Life-Changing Magic of Tidying Up.\n来认识一档叫《和近藤麻理惠一起整理》的电视节目，这是一档家居改善类节目，改编自她\n那本极受欢迎的书《怦然心动的人生整理魔法》。\n【信号】开头点题句直接报出节目名与定位。录音一开篇就用 a TV show called \"Tidying Up with\nMarie Kondo\", a home improvement show 点明节目名和它“家居改善”的定位，这是主旨\n题最典型的“开头点题”信号——主题在第一句就交代了。\n【替换】Tidying Up with Marie Kondo ↔ Tidying up one's home.\n节目名里的 Tidying Up 概括为“整理家”\na home improvement show ↔ Tidying up one's home.\nhome improvement = 整理 one's home\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 her wildly popular book 和 share the wisdom of the \"KonMari\" method，A\n把 book、wisdom 两个原词拼成 Reading books of wisdom.，但那本书只是节目的来\n源、wisdom 指整理方法的智慧，节目本身不是“读智慧之书”，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：share the wisdom of the \"KonMari\" method\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音说近藤 share the wisdom of the \"KonMari\" method，C 借 share 造出 Sharing\nwith others.，可原文 share 的宾语是“整理方法的智慧”，不是“和别人分享东西”，主题更不\n是分享，属答非所问的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：share the wisdom of the \"KonMari\" method\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音后半提到人们 bringing piles of donation bags to used goods stores，D 据此造出\nDonating to charity.，但捐赠只是节目带来的“效果”而非节目本身的主题，且原文是捐到二\n手店、并非 charity，既偷换概念又答非所问。\n借用原文：bringing piles of donation bags to used goods stores", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What things can be kept in one's home according to Marie Kondo?", "options": ["A) Things that make one happy.", "B) Things that are becoming rare.", "C) Things that occupy little space.", "D) Things that cost a lot of money."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Things that make one happy.\n【定位】...examine each item—all of them—to see if it sparks joy. If it does, you keep it.\n……逐一检视每件物品——全部都要——看它是否让你心动（带来喜悦）。如果会，你就留\n下它。\n【信号】条件句 If it does, you keep it 给出“留下”的标准。录音用 to see if it sparks joy. If it\ndoes, you keep it. 这个条件—结果句直接给出判断标准：会带来 joy 的就留下。这里\nkeep 与题干 kept 对应、joy 是判定信号，正是答案句。\n【替换】if it sparks joy ↔ Things that make one happy.\nsparks joy = make one happy\nyou keep it ↔ can be kept\nyou keep it 主动 = can be kept 被动\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音判定标准只有“是否 spark joy”，全程没有任何关于“东西稀有/将变稀有”的说法，\nThings that are becoming rare. 是凭空添加的标准。\nC) 未提及\n原文整理的标准是 joy，与物品占地大小无关；Things that occupy little space. 把一个常\n识里合理、但录音没说的标准塞进来，属未提及。\nD) 未提及\n近藤的标准是看是否 sparks joy，与价格无关，甚至贵而不让你心动的也该丢；Things\nthat cost a lot of money. 既非原文标准，又与“按心动取舍”的精神相左。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What do we learn about one used bookstore this January?", "options": ["A) It joined the city's clean-up campaign.", "B) It sold as many as fifty boxes of books.", "C) It received an incredibly large number of donated books.", "D) It did little business because of the unusual cold weather."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It received an incredibly large number of donated books.\n【定位】One used bookstore received a month's worth of books in donations in a week when a man\ngave over fifty boxes of books from his home.\n一家二手书店在一周内收到的捐书量相当于平常一个月，因为有一个人把家里五十多箱书都\n捐了出来。\n【信号】对象 + 时间复现，紧跟的动词 received 是落点。录音出现 One used bookstore\nreceived a month's worth of books in donations in a week，与题干的 used\nbookstore、January 对象时间吻合，紧跟的动词 received（收到）正是这家书店“发生了\n什么”的核心信号。\n【替换】received a month's worth of books in donations in a week ↔\nreceived an incredibly large number of donated books\n“一周收到一个月的量”=“收到大量捐书”\ngave over fifty boxes of books ↔ an incredibly large number of donated books\nfifty boxes 是“数量大”的实证\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音说 People seemed determined to clean up their homes，A 借 clean-up 造出 It\njoined the city's clean-up campaign.，可 clean up 的主语是“人们整理自家”，根本没说\n书店参加什么城市清理活动，属张冠李戴式原词陷阱。\n借用原文：People seemed determined to clean up their homes\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音原文是一个人 gave over fifty boxes of books（捐出五十多箱给书店），B 直接搬来\n数字造出 It sold as many as fifty boxes of books.，把动作从“收到捐赠”偷换成“书店卖\n出”，数字耳熟但方向全反，是最毒的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：gave over fifty boxes of books from his home\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说一月通常因为 it's cold and people don't want to bother 捐赠少，但 not this\nJanuary——今年反而捐赠暴增；D 造出 It did little business because of the unusual\ncold weather.，既偷用 cold，又取了被 but 否定掉的“前半”，与原文结论相反。\n借用原文：January is usually the stores' slow season for donations because it's cold ... but not\nthis January", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does Mike Hannon do to help people in his town?", "options": ["A) Give free meals to the homeless.", "B) Provide shelter for the homeless.", "C) Help the vulnerable to cook lunches.", "D) Call for change in the local government."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Give free meals to the homeless.\n【定位】\"Mike's Lunches of Love\" has fed more than 2,000 of the town's most vulnerable residents.\nMike delivers meals to the homeless.\n迈克的爱心午餐已经为镇上 2000 多名最弱势的居民提供了食物。迈克把饭菜送到无家可归\n者手中。\n【信号】反复出现的核心动作 fed / delivers meals。定位信号是这个动作被连说两遍且是全篇主线\n——先说 has fed more than 2,000，紧接着 Mike delivers meals to the homeless，喂\n饭、送饭反复强调，正是他帮人的核心行为，答案句就在这里。\n【替换】Mike delivers meals to the homeless ↔ Give free meals to the homeless.\ndelivers meals 同义改写为 Give free meals\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nProvide shelter for the homeless. 借用了原文里的 the homeless 这个原词来制造听到就\n想选的诱惑，但录音说的是送饭（meals），从没说提供住处（shelter），把帮助方式偷换\n了。\n借用原文：Mike delivers meals to the homeless\nC) 原词陷阱\nHelp the vulnerable to cook lunches. 偷用了原文 most vulnerable residents 的\nvulnerable 一词，且 lunches 让人联想到 Lunches of Love，听着很耳熟；但原文是他亲\n自送饭给弱势者，不是帮弱势者自己做午饭，动作主体和方式都被改了。\n借用原文：\"Mike's Lunches of Love\" has fed more than 2,000 of the town's most\nvulnerable residents.\nD) 原词陷阱\nCall for change in the local government. 里的 change 看似呼应原文 change them，但\n原文的 change 指改变受助者的内心，而非呼吁地方政府改革；原文通篇讲做善事，根本没\n提向政府提诉求。\n借用原文：spark something in them that can change them", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does Mike hope others will do?", "options": ["A) Strengthen co-operation.", "B) Promote understanding.", "C) Win national support.", "D) Following his example."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Following his example.\n【定位】He hopes his acts of charity will influence others to spread positive actions in their\nown towns.\n他希望自己的慈善之举能影响别人，让他们在自己的城镇也去传播积极的行动。\n【信号】愿望动词 hopes 直接领出期望。定位信号是表愿望的动词 He hopes——它后面那句 will\ninfluence others to spread positive actions 就是他希望别人做的事。听到 hope 立刻锁\n定其后内容，这是愿望类细节题的硬定位点。\n【替换】influence others to spread positive actions in their own towns ↔ Following his example.\n影响别人去做同样的事即效仿他\n【排除】A) 未提及\nStrengthen co-operation. 是个听着很正面的大词，但原文从没说他希望大家加强合作；他\n要的是别人各自在自己城镇行善（spread positive actions），不是彼此协作。属于原文未\n出现的内容。\nB) 未提及\nPromote understanding. 同样是个正面大词，原文没有任何关于增进理解的表述。他的愿\n望明确是 spread positive actions（传播积极行动），与增进理解不是一回事。\nC) 原词陷阱\nWin national support. 里的 national 容易呼应原文后文反复出现的 all over the\ncountry，让人听着耳熟；但赢得全国支持讲的是他自己声名远播的结果，不是他希望别人\n做的事，答非所问。\n借用原文：His message and star power have spread all over the country.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "How are people all over the country responding to what Mike is doing?", "options": ["A) Spreading news of his deeds.", "B) Writing him thank-you notes.", "C) Following the example he sets.", "D) Sending him hand-made bags."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Sending him hand-made bags.\n【定位】People from all over the country are sending special handcrafted bags to help the young man\nwith his mission to help those in need.\n全国各地的人们正在寄送特制的手工袋子，来帮助这个少年完成他帮助困难者的使命。\n【信号】他人动作进行时 People are sending。定位信号是描述他人正在做某事的进行时句\nPeople from all over the country are sending special handcrafted bags——题干问\nhow people are responding，原文这句正面回答了人们在做什么，是答案句。\n【替换】People from all over the country are sending special handcrafted bags ↔\nSending him hand-made bags.\nhandcrafted bags 同义为 hand-made bags\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nSpreading news of his deeds. 借用了原文多次出现的 spread（spread positive actions\n/ have spread all over the country）来制造耳熟，但原文 spread 的是积极行动和他的影\n响力，不是说人们在传播他事迹的新闻，答非所问。\n借用原文：His message and star power have spread all over the country.\nB) 张冠李戴\nWriting him thank-you notes. 容易让人想起原文 Mike includes a handwritten\nmessage——但写手写留言的是迈克自己（写在每个袋子上），不是别人给迈克写感谢信。\n这是把迈克做的事安到别人头上的张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Mike includes a handwritten message of joy on each bag.\nC) 答非所问\nFollowing the example he sets. 与 20 题答案 Following his example. 几乎同义，是迈克\n希望别人做的（his hope），不是别人实际的回应方式。原文讲别人实际在寄手工袋子，而\n非说人们已经在效仿他，把愿望偷换成了现实回应。\n借用原文：influence others to spread positive actions in their own towns", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What were the college students in both groups required to do in the study?", "options": ["A) To solve word search puzzles.", "B) To send smartphone messages.", "C) To test their eyesight using a phone app.", "D) To install some audio equipment in a lab."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） To solve word search puzzles.\n【定位】two groups of college students were given word search puzzles.\n两组大学生被分发了找词谜题（让他们解）。\n【信号】实验开头交代任务的句子。答案句是篇章开头交代实验安排的那句 two groups of college\nstudents were given word search puzzles，‘被给了找词谜题’正是两组共同要做的事；细\n节题的答案句往往就落在交代任务/背景的起始句，听到 were given 这种安排动作即锁定。\n【替换】were given word search puzzles ↔ To solve word search puzzles.\n被给谜题=去解谜题\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nTo send smartphone messages. 把原文反复出现的 smartphones 拿来装样子，但原文里\n手机只是被‘放在视线内/挪走/打进来响’的实验变量，从没让学生去‘发短信’，是听到\nsmartphone 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：smartphones in their line of sight\nC) 未提及\nTo test their eyesight using a phone app. 听起来像个合理实验，但原文研究的是手机对\n‘注意力/专注’的干扰，全篇没提 eyesight 或 app，属凭常识臆造的未提及项。\nD) 原词陷阱\nTo install some audio equipment in a lab. 借了原文第二组被告知手机会 interfere with\nthe equipment 里的 equipment 一词，但 equipment 是实验里的‘设备’、是手机要被挪\n开的理由，绝非让学生去‘安装音频设备’，把无关动作硬安在学生头上。\n借用原文：would interfere with the equipment in the experiment", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What do we learn about many of the students in the second test group after the phone rang?", "options": ["A) They could not go on until the ringing stopped.", "B) They could no longer concentrate on their task.", "C) They grabbed the phone and called back right away.", "D) They asked their experimenter to hang up the phone."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They could no longer concentrate on their task.\n【定位】Many of the students in that group were unable to focus from then on, becoming anxious and\nperforming more poorly than the first group.\n那一组里许多学生从那以后再也无法集中注意力，变得焦虑，表现比第一组更差。\n【信号】事件之后紧跟的状态描述句。电话响（let it ring for a while before hanging up）这个事\n件之后紧接的描述句就是答案句：Many of the students in that group were unable to\nfocus from then on。细节题里‘某事件之后怎样’的答案，几乎都落在事件句的下一句，听\n到 from then on 这种时间衔接就锁定。\n【替换】were unable to focus from then on ↔ They could no longer concentrate on their task.\n无法集中=不再能专注\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey could not go on until the ringing stopped. 借了原文 let it ring for a while 的\nring，编成‘要等铃声停了才能继续’。但原文是‘从那以后一直无法专注’，并非‘等铃停就好\n了’，恰恰相反——干扰是持续的，这是盗用 ring 又改了意思的陷阱。\n借用原文：let it ring for a while before hanging up\nC) 未提及\nThey grabbed the phone and called back right away. 描述学生抓起电话立刻回拨，原文\n里学生根本没碰电话（是 experimenter 打进来再挂掉），学生只是变得焦虑、表现变差，\n‘回拨’是凭想象添的动作。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey asked their experimenter to hang up the phone. 借了原文 experimenter 和\nhang up（before hanging up），但挂电话的是实验者本人主动挂的，不是学生要求他挂\n的，把动作的发起方安错了人。\n借用原文：before hanging up", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "According to the passage, what is the most dramatic impact of smartphone use?", "options": ["A) A rise in emotional problems.", "B) A decline in sports activities.", "C) A reduction in the amount of sleep.", "D) A decline in academic performance."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） A reduction in the amount of sleep.\n【定位】Perhaps the most dramatic impact is the reduction in the amount of sleep, which leads to poor\nhealth and weight gain.\n也许最显著的影响是睡眠量的减少，它会导致健康变差和体重增加。\n【信号】最高级the most dramatic直接点题。答案句被最高级标记直接点亮：Perhaps the most\ndramatic impact is the reduction in the amount of sleep，与题干 the most dramatic\nimpact 完全对应。听力里最高级 the most… 后面紧跟的就是答案，是最硬的定位信号。\n【替换】the reduction in the amount of sleep ↔ A reduction in the amount of sleep.\n几乎原句搬到选项\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA rise in emotional problems. 蹭了第二段 express emotions 里的 emotion，但原文那\n句讲的是人们改用社交媒体表达情绪、并未说情绪问题‘上升’，更没把它列为‘最突出’的影\n响，属盗用 emotion 一词的陷阱。\n借用原文：many express emotions and engage in deep conversations through social media sites\nB) 未提及\nA decline in sports activities. 说体育活动减少，原文只提到 human interactions（人际\n互动）减少，从未提及 sports，属凭常识联想（手机多→运动少）添出来的未提及项。\nD) 答非所问\nA decline in academic performance. 对应原文 leads to poor grades，学业下滑原文确\n实提了，但它只是并列后果之一、没被冠以 the most dramatic，被最高级点名的是睡眠，\n故D是真有其事却答错重点的诱选。\n借用原文：which leads to poor grades", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker suggest people do?", "options": ["A) Protect the eyesight of the younger generation.", "B) Take effective measures to raise productivity.", "C) Realize the disruptive effects of technology.", "D) Ensure they have sufficient sleep every day."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） Realize the disruptive effects of technology.\n【定位】Technology is a great tool; however, it's important to recognize its downsides.\n科技是个很好的工具；然而，认清它的负面作用很重要。\n【信号】转折however后的主张句。答案句藏在转折之后：先扬 Technology is a great tool，转折\nhowever, it's important to recognize its downsides 才是说话人真正要强调的主张。转折\nhowever 后、又带 it's important to 这种态度口吻的句子，正是说话人立场的落点，是目\n的题最强的信号。\n【替换】recognize its downsides ↔ Realize the disruptive effects of technology.\n认清负面=意识到破坏性影响\n【排除】A) 未提及\nProtect the eyesight of the younger generation. 拼了原文末句 the future generation\n的 generation，但 protect eyesight（保护视力）原文从没提，是借 generation 一词又\n凭常识添出来的未提及项。\n借用原文：the effect on the future generation\nB) 原词陷阱\nTake effective measures to raise productivity. 借了第三段 reduction of productivity 的\nproductivity，但原文把 productivity 下降列为科技的‘坏处之一’，说话人的主张是‘认清这\n些坏处’，而非‘采取措施提高生产力’，盗词又跑题。\n借用原文：reduction of productivity\nD) 答非所问\nEnsure they have sufficient sleep every day. 蹭了第二、三段反复出现的 sleep，但‘保证\n充足睡眠’只是科技危害里的一个具体侧面，说话人结尾给的是总括主张‘认清科技的负面’，\nD把局部当成了说话人的整体建议。\n借用原文：Lack of sleep, reduction of productivity and weight gain are only a few.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appearance", "B) argued", "C) avoid", "D) considerable", "E) definitely", "F) extreme", "G) inaction", "H) incredibly", "I) normal", "J) possibly", "K) proposition", "L) repelled", "M) statement", "N) tend", "O) traditional"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) normal\n形容词\n【词性槽】形容词修饰名词短语 ups and downs\n【依据】normal ups and downs = 正常的起伏\nthe normal ups and downs of investment markets = 投资市场『正常的』起起落落——\n这是为下文做铺垫：正是这种『本属正常』的波动让某些人焦虑，凸显其抗压力不足\n（unable or unprepared to endure the long haul）。\n【竞争词】D) considerable\nconsiderable『相当大的』把锅推给市场，与下句『这些人无法忍受长期持有』的主旨方向\n相反\n如果填 considerable，意思变成『市场剧烈起伏让人焦虑』，那就是合理反应，与下句强调\n『这些人本身不耐长期持有』的主旨矛盾——作者要说的是『连正常波动他们都受不了』。且\nD considerable 留给 28 题做 a considerable percentage 才是命题人最常用的搭配（见第\n28 题）。\nO) traditional traditional『传统的』与 ups and downs 搭配不自然\n市场的起伏一般用 normal/regular/usual 描述其常态，traditional 多用于传统做法/传统行业，\n与『市场波动』搭配生硬。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 D) —— considerable 看似合理实则反主旨。the __(26)__ ups and\ndowns of investment markets 处不少考生会填 considerable『相当大的』，把锅推给市\n场剧烈波动；但下句 These people are usually unable or unprepared to endure the\nlong haul. 明确指出问题在『当事人不耐长期持有』，所以这里应是 normal『正常的』\n——连正常波动都受不了，才凸显当事人抗压不足。防坑：形容词题先看作者立场指向当事\n人还是指向外部。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appearance", "B) argued", "C) avoid", "D) considerable", "E) definitely", "F) extreme", "G) inaction", "H) incredibly", "I) normal", "J) possibly", "K) proposition", "L) repelled", "M) statement", "N) tend", "O) traditional"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) definitely\n副词\n【词性槽】not so much A but ___ B 平行结构里修饰名词性短语\n【依据】definitely 强调『肯定就是 the downs』，与 not so much the ups 形成强对比\nnot so much the ups, but definitely the downs = 与其说是上涨让他们焦虑，不如说\n『肯定就是』下跌让他们焦虑——definitely 的『肯定地』语气与 not so much 的『不那\n么』形成精准对比。\n【竞争词】H) incredibly incredibly 多修饰形容词/副词，且语义『极其』程度过头\nincredibly 常用法是 incredibly + adj./adv.（如 incredibly fast）；这里直接修饰名词短语 the\ndowns 不自然，且『难以置信地』与『肯定地』在此对比框架下力度也不对。\nJ) possibly possibly『可能』削弱语气，与 but 引导的强对比相悖\nbut 后通常是『肯定/强调』的转折，填 possibly『可能是 the downs』反而让对比软掉，逻辑\n也变成『也许是』，违背作者要强调下跌才是压力源的意图。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 J) —— possibly 削弱语气与 but 强对比相悖。not so much the\nups, but __(27)__ the downs 处看到 possibly 觉得『可能就是 the downs』也通，但 but\n引导的是强对比，副词应是强化方向（definitely），不是削弱方向（possibly）。防坑：\nbut 后填副词时优先确定『强化』还是『弱化』。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appearance", "B) argued", "C) avoid", "D) considerable", "E) definitely", "F) extreme", "G) inaction", "H) incredibly", "I) normal", "J) possibly", "K) proposition", "L) repelled", "M) statement", "N) tend", "O) traditional"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) considerable\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 与名词 percentage 之间填形容词\n【依据】a considerable percentage of 是『相当大比例的』固定搭配\nIn a considerable percentage of cases of debt-induced financial stress, credit cards\nand loans will be a central element. = 『相当大比例的债务诱发型财务压力案例中，信用\n卡和贷款是核心要素』——considerable 用来强调比例『可观』，正好支撑作者后续把信\n用卡定位为『债务困境的入口』(credit cards often seem to be the gateway to debt-\nrelated financial difficulties)。\n【竞争词】O) traditional traditional percentage 不是英语固定搭配，语义也不通\n『传统的比例』不是常用说法，比例只有大/小、相当大/可忽略等量化形容词；traditional 在这\n里完全没有量化含义。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appearance", "B) argued", "C) avoid", "D) considerable", "E) definitely", "F) extreme", "G) inaction", "H) incredibly", "I) normal", "J) possibly", "K) proposition", "L) repelled", "M) statement", "N) tend", "O) traditional"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) possibly\n副词\n【词性槽】句中插入语，修饰整个判断『最不为人知』\n【依据】possibly『也许是最不为人知的』口吻克制、合乎学术叙述\nand possibly the least known = 『而且可能也是最不为人知的一类』，作者用 possibly\n表达留有余地的判断；与紧接其后的强判断 which is the most destructive『最具破坏性』\n在语气上一软一硬，恰好搭配。\n【竞争词】H) incredibly incredibly『难以置信地』与 the least known 搭配不自然\n『难以置信地最不为人知』语义别扭；且 incredibly 通常修饰形容词时强调程度（incredibly\nhard），与 the least known 这种最高级判断重叠拗口。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appearance", "B) argued", "C) avoid", "D) considerable", "E) definitely", "F) extreme", "G) inaction", "H) incredibly", "I) normal", "J) possibly", "K) proposition", "L) repelled", "M) statement", "N) tend", "O) traditional"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) argued\n动词\n【词性槽】情态副词 regularly 后 + 与 and fought 并列\n【依据】argue and fight 是常见搭配，描述父母为钱争吵打架\n代入 their parents regularly argued and fought about money.，意为『父母经常为钱争\n吵和打架』；与下句 Money therefore becomes a stressful topic 因果连贯——父母吵闹\n→ 孩子心里把钱当压力话题。\n【竞争词】L) repelled repel 是『击退/使厌恶』，不能描述父母之间的争吵\nrepel 一般用作 repel sb./sth.（带宾语），且语义偏『驱赶/令人反感』，与下句 fought about\nmoney（为钱打架）的对象一致性、语义递进都对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appearance", "B) argued", "C) avoid", "D) considerable", "E) definitely", "F) extreme", "G) inaction", "H) incredibly", "I) normal", "J) possibly", "K) proposition", "L) repelled", "M) statement", "N) tend", "O) traditional"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) proposition\n名词\n【词性槽】be 动词 is 后 + 形容词 unattractive 修饰 → 表语名词\n【依据】an unattractive proposition = 一件没有吸引力的事/提议\nthe thought of sitting down and planning is an unattractive proposition. = 『坐下来\n好好做财务规划这件事，对他们来说是没有吸引力的提议/选项』。proposition 在这里取\n『事情，提议』之意，与 the thought of sitting down and planning（坐下来规划的念\n头）正好对应：作者把『规划』视作一个可接受/不接受的选项。\n【竞争词】A) appearance appearance『外表/出现』与『规划是一件无吸引力之事』不搭\nan unattractive appearance 通常指『一个不吸引人的外表』，主语是人或物的外观，不能用来\n形容『规划这个念头』。\nG) inaction inaction『不作为』与『坐下来规划』语义相反\n规划是『行动』，把它说成 unattractive inaction（无吸引力的不作为）逻辑直接打架。\nM) statement statement『声明/报表』与『一个念头』不对应\nan unattractive statement 是『一份不吸引人的声明』，话题对象错位；且 statement 留作他\n用（在第 33 题里出现的 financial statements 已说明它指『财务报表』）。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appearance", "B) argued", "C) avoid", "D) considerable", "E) definitely", "F) extreme", "G) inaction", "H) incredibly", "I) normal", "J) possibly", "K) proposition", "L) repelled", "M) statement", "N) tend", "O) traditional"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) tend\n动词\n【词性槽】主语后缺谓语 + 后接 to do\n【依据】tend to do 固定搭配 = 倾向于\n代入 Those suffering inherited financial anxiety tend to follow one of two patterns. =\n这些人『倾向于』遵循两种模式之一，语义贴合下文紧接的 Either ... Alternatively ... 两种\n模式分述。\n【竞争词】B) argued 过去式不合时态 + 不接 to do\nargued 是过去式，与全文一般现在时叙述（There are three main types）不符，且 argue to\ndo 不是常用搭配。\nC) avoid avoid 后接动名词不接不定式\navoid 的搭配是 avoid doing，不是 avoid to do；语法上直接出局。\nL) repelled 过去式 + 词义不符\nrepelled 是过去式形态；意思是『击退/使厌恶』，与『遵循两种模式之一』语义不搭。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appearance", "B) argued", "C) avoid", "D) considerable", "E) definitely", "F) extreme", "G) inaction", "H) incredibly", "I) normal", "J) possibly", "K) proposition", "L) repelled", "M) statement", "N) tend", "O) traditional"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) avoid\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 would 后接动词原形 + 后接动名词宾语\n【依据】avoid doing 固定搭配 + 与『put their head in the sand』呼应\n上句 Either they put their head in the sand（把头埋进沙里 = 鸵鸟式回避）已经定调\n『回避』，本空选 avoid 与之意思一致：他们会回避查看财务报表、做预算、讨论财务。\n【竞争词】L) repelled 形态不合 + 搭配不对\nrepelled 是过去式/过去分词形态，不能跟在 would 后（would 后应接原形 repel）；且 repel\n意为『击退/排斥某人某物』，repel doing 不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appearance", "B) argued", "C) avoid", "D) considerable", "E) definitely", "F) extreme", "G) inaction", "H) incredibly", "I) normal", "J) possibly", "K) proposition", "L) repelled", "M) statement", "N) tend", "O) traditional"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) extreme\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 other 与介词 and 之间应为名词\n【依据】go to the other extreme = 走向另一个极端，固定搭配\nAlternatively, they would go to the other extreme, and micro-analyze everything, to\nthe point of complete __(35)__.：上文 Either 端是『鸵鸟式回避』，下文 Alternatively\n端是『微观分析一切』——正是两个极端。go to the other extreme 这条固定短语正好把\n两端结构封住。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) appearance", "B) argued", "C) avoid", "D) considerable", "E) definitely", "F) extreme", "G) inaction", "H) incredibly", "I) normal", "J) possibly", "K) proposition", "L) repelled", "M) statement", "N) tend", "O) traditional"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) inaction\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 complete 修饰 + 介词短语 to the point of ___ 作宾语\n【依据】micro-analyze 到完全 inaction = 分析到瘫痪不作为（analysis paralysis）\nmicro-analyze everything, to the point of complete inaction. = 『把一切都细到极致地\n分析，以至于完全无法行动』——这正是『分析瘫痪』的经典描述，与下句 They are\nconvinced that whatever decision they make will be the wrong one.『他们认定无论做\n什么决定都是错的』完美呼应：因怕错而不动。\n【竞争词】A) appearance appearance『外表』与『过度分析到极致』毫无逻辑关系\ncomplete appearance（完全的外表）不构成可懂表达，话题完全对不上。\nM) statement complete statement 与上下文『分析到无法行动』脱节\n『完全声明』在语境里无指向；且本段强调的是行动层面的瘫痪，不是表态层面的完整。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Increased use of digital devices steals away children's playtime.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】数字设备偷走玩耍时间\n【定位】Another playtime thief: the growing proportion of kids' time spent in front of screens and\ndigital devices, even among preschoolers.\n另一个偷走玩耍时间的「贼」是：儿童花在屏幕和数字设备前的时间比例越来越高，连学龄\n前儿童也不例外。\n【改写】Increased use of digital devices ↔\nthe growing proportion of kids' time spent in front of screens and digital devices\n名词短语等价转写\nsteals away children's playtime ↔ Another playtime thief\n比喻的动词化还原\n【辨邻】 I I 段虽同样谈 hand a child an iPhone 这种数字设备使用，但讨论的是 it's much too\npassive 这种被动性后果，并未提「挤压/偷走玩耍时间」，覆盖局部。\nI respect that parents have busy lives and it's easy to hand a child an iPhone", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Since the beginning of this century, an increasing amount of time has been shifted in public schools from recess to academic activities.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】本世纪初课间被学业挤压\n【定位】Since the adoption of sweeping education reforms in 2001, public schools have steadily\nincreased the amount of time devoted to preparing for standardized tests. The focus on\nacademic \"skills and drills\" has cut deeply into recess(课间休息) and other time for free play.\n自 2001 年大规模教育改革以来，公立学校用于备战标准化考试的时间稳步增加。这种对学\n业「技能与训练」的聚焦，深深挤占了课间和其它自由玩耍的时间。\n【改写】Since the beginning of this century ↔\nSince the adoption of sweeping education reforms in 2001\n时间范围的上位概括\nan increasing amount of time has been shifted in public schools from recess to academic activities\n↔\npublic schools have steadily increased the amount of time devoted to preparing for\nstandardized tests\n增减表达对位\n【辨邻】 F F 段同样谈学业压力挤课间，但给的是 By 2009 的截面结果，并未陈述「自本世纪初\n起就开始转移」这一持续动作。\nBy 2009, a study of Los Angeles kindergarten classrooms found that five-year-olds were so\nburdened with academic requirements", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "It has been acknowledged that while kids may welcome pediatricians' recommendation, their parents may doubt its feasibility.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】孩子欢迎、家长怀疑可行性\n【定位】But UCLA pediatrician Carlos Lerner acknowledged that the pediatricians' new prescription\nmay meet with skepticism(怀疑) from parents, who are anxious for advice on how to give\ntheir kids a leg up in the world.\n但 UCLA 儿科医生 Carlos Lerner 承认，儿科医生开出的这条新「处方」可能遭到家长的怀\n疑——这些家长正急切地想知道怎样让自己的孩子在世界上占得先机。\n【改写】kids may welcome pediatricians' recommendation ↔ Play may not be a hard sell to kids\n否定加商业用语的换说\ntheir parents may doubt its feasibility ↔\nthe pediatricians' new prescription may meet with skepticism(怀疑) from parents\n近义词替换\n【辨邻】 C C 段虽然也讲家长反应 may come as a shock to some parents，但「震惊」是初次\n听到的反应，与题干「怀疑可行性」的语义不一致；且 C 段未陈述「孩子欢迎而家长\n怀疑」这一两端反差结构。\nThe advice, issued Monday by the American Academy of Pediatrics, may come as a shock to\nsome parents", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "According to some professionals, deprivation of young children's playtime will do harm not only to children themselves but to the country and the world.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】对孩子、国家与世界都有害\n【定位】In a report titled \"Crisis in the Kindergarten,\" a group of educators, health professionals and\nchild advocates called the loss of play in early childhood \"a tragedy, both for the children\nthemselves and for our nation and world.\"\n在一份名为《幼儿园危机》的报告中，一群教育工作者、健康专业人士与儿童权益倡导者将\n幼儿期玩耍的流失称为「一场悲剧——既是对孩子本身的，也是对我们国家和世界的」。\n【改写】According to some professionals ↔\na group of educators, health professionals and child advocates\n上位词替换\ndeprivation of young children's playtime will do harm not only to children themselves but to the\ncountry and the world\n↔\nthe loss of play in early childhood a tragedy, both for the children themselves and for our\nnation and world\n三元并列的同义替换\n【辨邻】 D D 段虽然提到 harmful effects of all kinds of stress 涉及伤害，但说话主体是 the\nacademy 而非题干所指的 some professionals；并且 D 段未出现「孩子 + 国家 + 世\n界」的三元并列。\nWhen parents engage in play with their children, it builds a wall against the harmful effects of all\nkinds of stress", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "By playing with children, parents can prevent them from being harmed by stress.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】家长陪玩防压力之害\n【定位】When parents engage in play with their children, it builds a wall against the harmful effects of\nall kinds of stress, including poverty, the academy says.\n学会指出，当家长与孩子一起玩耍时，能筑起一道墙，抵御各种压力（包括贫困）所带来的\n危害。\n【改写】By playing with children, parents ↔ When parents engage in play with their children\n状语换成时间状语从句\ncan prevent them from being harmed by stress ↔\nbuilds a wall against the harmful effects of all kinds of stress\n比喻与字面表达防止\n【辨邻】 J J 段陈述 The decline of play is a special hazard for the roughly 1 in 5 children in\nthe United States who live in poverty，主语是「玩耍流失对贫困儿童的危害」，与\n题干「家长陪玩到防止压力伤害」方向相反。\nThe decline of play is a special hazard for the roughly 1 in 5 children in the United States who live\nin poverty", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Playing with digital devices discourages kids from active discovery, according to pediatrician Dr. Michael Yogman.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Yogman 论数字设备抑制主动探索\n【定位】But there's a cost to that. For young children, it's much too passive. And kids really learn\nbetter when they're actively engaged and have to really discover things.\n但这是有代价的。对幼儿来说，这种方式过于被动；孩子真正学得好，是在他们被主动调\n动、并且必须自己去发现东西的时候。\n【改写】Playing with digital devices ↔ hand a child an iPhone\n下位例子做上位指代\ndiscourages kids from active discovery ↔\nit's much too passive. And kids really learn better when they're actively engaged and have to\nreally discover things\n负面动作等于正面缺位\naccording to pediatrician Dr. Michael Yogman ↔ Yogman said\n言说主体专名复现\n【辨邻】 H H 段虽然也讲数字设备危害 rising risks of obesity, sleep deprivation and\ncognitive(认知的), language and social-emotional delays，但说话主体是 the\nAmerican Academy of Pediatrics warned in 2016，不是 Yogman 个人发言。\nThis increase of digital use comes with rising risks of obesity, sleep deprivation and cognitive(认知\n的), language and social-emotional delays, the American Academy of Pediatrics warned in 2016", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The suggestion of letting children simply play may sound like going backwards to parents who want to help build their children's skills.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】让孩子单纯玩听起来像倒退\n【定位】After spending years fretting(烦恼) over which toys to buy, which apps to download and\nwhich skill-building programs to send their kids to after school, letting them simply play—or\nbetter yet, playing with them—could seem like a step backward.\n在多年纠结于买什么玩具、下哪些应用、把孩子送去哪个课后技能班之后，让孩子单纯地玩\n（或者更进一步，陪他们一起玩）可能听上去像在开倒车。\n【改写】letting children simply play ↔ letting them simply play\n原样复现\nparents who want to help build their children's skills ↔\nwhich skill-building programs to send their kids to after school\n动机的具象化\ngoing backwards ↔ a step backward\n近义比喻替换\n【辨邻】 L L 段虽然也谈家长对建议的反应 meet with skepticism(怀疑) from parents，但情绪\n是「怀疑」，并未表达「觉得是倒退」这一具体感受。\nthe pediatricians' new prescription may meet with skepticism(怀疑) from parents, who are anxious\nfor advice on how to give their kids a leg up in the world", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Dr. Michael Yogman believes the idea that parents should carefully schedule children's time may not be helpful to their growth.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Yogman 反对把孩子时间排满\n【定位】\"The notion that as parents we need to schedule every minute of their time is not doing them\na great service,\" he said.\n「我们做家长的需要把孩子每一分钟时间都安排好」，这种观念其实并未真正帮到他们，\n他说。\n【改写】parents should carefully schedule children's time ↔\nas parents we need to schedule every minute of their time\n同义动词加程度词强化\nmay not be helpful to their growth ↔ is not doing them a great service\n礼貌否定与直白否定\n【辨邻】 I I 段 Yogman 的话题是 it's easy to hand a child an iPhone 与「主动 vs 被动」问\n题，而题干谈的是「安排时间」，主题不一致。\nFor young children, it's much too passive. And kids really learn better when they're actively\nengaged and have to really discover things", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "One quarter of teachers in an American city said that children in kindergartens had no time for playing freely.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】某城市四分之一教师称无自由玩耍\n【定位】One in four Los Angeles teachers reported there was no time at all for \"free play.\"\n四分之一的洛杉矶教师称，孩子完全没有「自由玩耍」的时间。\n【改写】One quarter of teachers in an American city ↔ One in four Los Angeles teachers\n比例与地名上位替换\nchildren in kindergartens had no time for playing freely ↔ there was no time at all for free play\n副词与形容词形式互换\n【辨邻】 E E 段虽然也谈玩耍时间下降 the time children spent at play declined by 25\npercent，给的是「25%」这一整体下降幅度，不是「四分之一教师称无自由玩耍」这\n一具体指标。\nBetween 1981 and 1997, detailed time-use studies showed that the time children spent at play\ndeclined by 25 percent", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "According to a pediatrician, no matter what kind of play children engage in, they are learning how to create things.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】无论玩哪种玩都是在学创造\n【定位】Whether it's rough physical play, outdoor play or social or pretend play, kids derive important\nlessons from the chance to make things up as they go, he said.\n他说，无论是激烈的身体玩耍、户外玩耍，还是社交或假装玩耍，孩子都能从「边玩边自己\n编造」的机会中学到重要的本领。\n【改写】no matter what kind of play children engage in ↔\nWhether it's rough physical play, outdoor play or social or pretend play\n概括与枚举\nthey are learning how to create things ↔\nkids derive important lessons from the chance to make things up as they go\n近义短语替换\n【辨邻】 D D 段虽然也谈玩耍培养的能力 Collaboration, negotiation, decision-making,\ncreativity, leadership, and increased physical activity，但并未用「无论哪种玩耍」\n这种并列结构来概括，不符题干 no matter what kind of play 的限定。\nCollaboration, negotiation, decision-making, creativity, leadership, and increased physical activity\nare just some of the skills and benefits children gain through play", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author say may have an adverse impact on people?", "options": ["A) Undergoing plastic surgeries in pursuit of beauty.", "B) Imitating the lifestyles of heroes and role models.", "C) Striving to achieve perfection regardless of financial cost.", "D) Attempting to meet society's expectation of appearance."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say may have an adverse impact on people? 用 What 直\n问作者所述的某种不利影响，是对原文事实信息的复述，不要求推理。\n【拆句】What does the author say may have an adverse impact on people? 主干其实是 What\nmay have an adverse impact on people（什么会对人产生不利影响），中间的 the\nauthor say 是插入说明「作者所说的」。考生若把 say 误当作主干动词，就会读不通；记\n住「主语+情态动词 have+宾语」才是句子骨架。\n【定位】Trying to live up to the images of \"perfect\" models and movie heroes has a dark side: anxiety\nand depression, as well as unhealthy strategies for weight loss or muscle gain.\n努力达到「完美」模特和电影英雄的形象是有阴暗面的：焦虑、抑郁，以及不健康的减肥或\n增肌方式。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nUndergoing plastic surgeries in pursuit of beauty（为了美而整形）只是 plastic\nsurgery 在引子段被列举的现象之一，原文没说「整形」本身有 dark side，而是说「试\n图迎合完美形象」才有。\nB) 偷换概念\nImitating the lifestyles of heroes and role models 偷偷把原文的 images（形象、外\n貌）替换成 lifestyles（生活方式），与原文 images of \"perfect\" models and movie\nheroes 强调的是「外貌形象」而非生活方式不符。\nC) 无中生有\nStriving to achieve perfection regardless of financial cost 的 regardless of\nfinancial cost（不计经济成本）在原文里没有依据；It also has a financial cost 只是说\n「同时也产生经济成本」，并非「不计成本」。\nD) ✓ 正确\nTrying to live up to the images of \"perfect\" models and movie heroes ↔\nAttempting to meet society's expectation of appearance\nAttempting to meet society's expectation of appearance 恰好把预判「迎合社会对\n外貌期待」逐字呈现，正中。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What have researchers found out about people's earnings?", "options": ["A) They are closely related to people's social status.", "B) They have to do with people's body weight and shape.", "C) They seem to matter much less to men than to women.", "D) They may not be equal to people's contributions."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What have researchers found out about people's earnings? 问研究者「发现了什\n么」，定位到结论句直接复述即可。\n【拆句】What have researchers found out about people's earnings? 现在完成时强调「已经研\n究并得出结论」，主干是 What have researchers found out（研究者发现了什么），\nabout people's earnings 是话题限定。结构平直，无陷阱。\n【定位】Researchers have shown that body mass index is related to wages and income.\n研究者已证明，身体质量指数（即体型胖瘦的衡量指标）与薪资和收入相关。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey are closely related to people's social status 的 social status（社会地位）在原\n文从未出现，原文只谈体重/体型与收入的关系。\nB) ✓ 正确\nbody mass index is related to wages and income ↔\nThey have to do with people's body weight and shape\nThey have to do with people's body weight and shape 把定位句逐成分等值改写，\n是与预判最贴合的一项。\nC) 答非所问\nThey seem to matter much less to men than to women 把比较关系错置在「收入」\n上，原文说的是「体重对女性收入的惩罚比男性明显」，而非「收入对男性意义更\n小」。题目问的是研究者发现的关于收入的事，而非男女对收入的看重程度。\nD) 无中生有\nThey may not be equal to people's contributions（收入可能与贡献不匹配）涉及的\n「贡献」概念在原文完全不存在。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the author's recent study focus on?", "options": ["A) Previous literature on indicators of competitiveness in the workplace.", "B) Traits that matter most in one's pursuit of success in the labor market.", "C) Whether self-perception of body image impacts one's workplace success.", "D) How bosses' perception of body image impacts employees' advancement."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author's recent study focus on? 直问研究焦点，需要在原文找到明\n确指明研究问题的句子。\n【拆句】What does the author's recent study focus on? 主干 What does the study focus on\n（研究关注什么），author's recent 是修饰 study 的两个前置定语。结构清晰，无难点。\n【定位】Does a person's own perception of body image matter to earnings and other indicators of\nsuccess in the workplace? Our recently published study answered this question by tracking a\nlarge national random sample of Americans over a critical time period when bodies change\nfrom teenage shape into adult form and when people build their identities.\n一个人自己对身体形象的感知是否影响其收入和其他职场成功指标？我们最近发表的研究通\n过追踪一批庞大的全国随机样本回答了这个问题——这些样本横跨人从青少年体型变成成年\n体型、并开始建立自我认同的关键时期。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nPrevious literature on indicators of competitiveness in the workplace 写的是「前\n人文献」，但原文明确说「前人文献清楚雇主感知会影响职场成功，无人探究问题的另\n一面」——前人文献是背景而非作者研究焦点。\nB) 无中生有\nTraits that matter most in one's pursuit of success in the labor market 的 Traits\n（特质）一词原文未出现；原文讨论的是「自我感知」这一具体维度，而非泛泛的\n「特质」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nDoes a person's own perception of body image matter to earnings and other indicators of success\nin the workplace?\n↔ Whether self-perception of body image impacts one's workplace success\nWhether self-perception of body image impacts one's workplace success 把 P4 的\n疑问句逐成分改写，预判一对就中。\nD) 与原文相反\nHow bosses' perception of body image impacts employees' advancement 的\nbosses' perception（雇主的感知）恰恰是原文 how employers and customers\nperceive your body image 这条已被前人研究透、作者明确表态「另一面没人探讨」的\n方向。作者研究焦点正是其对立面。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is the finding of the author's recent research?", "options": ["A) Being overweight actually does not do much harm to the overall well-being of employees.", "B) People are not adversely affected in the workplace by false self-perception of body weight.", "C) Self-esteem helps to combat gender inequality in the workplace.", "D) Gender inequality continues to frustrate a lot of female employees."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the finding of the author's recent research? 问研究结论本身，是对原文\nWe found... 句的同义复述。\n【拆句】What is the finding of the author's recent research? 主干 What is the finding（结果是\n什么），of the author's recent research 是后置定语限定 finding 的来源。无难点。\n【定位】We found no relationship between the average person's self-perception of weight and labor\nmarket outcomes, although self-perceived weight can influence self-esteem(自尊心), mental\nhealth and health behaviors.\n我们发现普通人对自身体重的感知与劳动力市场结果之间没有关系，尽管自我感知的体重确\n实会影响自尊心、心理健康和健康行为。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nBeing overweight actually does not do much harm to the overall well-being of\nemployees 把研究主语从「自我感知体重」换成「真实超重」；原文研究的是 self-\nperception（自我感知），不是 actual overweight；且原文从未说真实超重「不损\n害」员工，P3 反而明确女性超重在职场上有 clear penalty。\nB) ✓ 正确\nno relationship between the average person's self-perception of weight and labor market\noutcomes\n↔\nPeople are not adversely affected in the workplace by false self-perception of body weight\nPeople are not adversely affected in the workplace by false self-perception of\nbody weight 是对 P7 结论句的标准被动改写，与预判完全一致。\nC) 与原文相反\nSelf-esteem helps to combat gender inequality in the workplace 说「自尊心帮助\n对抗性别不平等」，与原文 self-perceived weight can influence self-esteem（自我\n感知体重影响自尊）方向完全相反，且原文没说自尊能对抗性别不平等。\nD) 答非所问\nGender inequality continues to frustrate a lot of female employees 对应的是 the\ncontinued gender penalty in the labor market is frustrating 那句情绪表态，并非作\n者本次研究的核心发现；题干问 finding（研究结论），不是问情绪感受。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author think would help improve the condition in the labor market?", "options": ["A) Banning discrimination on the basis of employees' body image.", "B) Expanding protection of women against gender discrimination.", "C) Helping employees change their own perception of beauty.", "D) Excluding body shape as a category in the labor contract."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author think would help improve the condition in the labor\nmarket? 问作者认为什么能改善现状，对应原文末段的明确主张句。\n【拆句】What does the author think would help improve the condition in the labor market?\n主干 What would help improve the condition（什么能帮助改善现状），the author\nthink 插入说明「作者认为」，in the labor market 限定改善对象。结构稍长但平直，提醒\n考生把 the author think 当插入语处理即可。\n【定位】Since employers' perception of weight is what matters in the labor market, changing\ndiscrimination laws to include body type as a category would also help. Michigan is the only\nstate that prohibits discrimination on the basis of weight and height. We believe expanding\nsuch protections would make the labor market more fair and efficient.\n既然在劳动力市场中真正起作用的是雇主对体重的感知，那么修改反歧视法、把体型也列入\n受保护类别就会有所帮助。密歇根州是唯一以法律禁止基于体重和身高进行歧视的州。我们\n相信扩大此类保护会让劳动力市场更公平、更高效。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nchanging discrimination laws to include body type as a category ↔\nBanning discrimination on the basis of employees' body image\nMichigan is the only state that prohibits discrimination on the basis of weight and height ↔\nBanning discrimination on the basis of employees' body image\nBanning discrimination on the basis of employees' body image 把 P9 的法律建议\n浓缩为一句，预判对比即中。\nB) 偷换概念\nExpanding protection of women against gender discrimination 把保护对象偷偷换\n成「女性免受性别歧视」，但原文末段主张扩大的是「基于体型的反歧视保护」（body\ntype），不是性别歧视保护；P8 提到 gender penalty 但作者紧接着说 our finding...\nis more heartening，并未把性别保护作为主推方案。\nC) 与原文相反\nHelping employees change their own perception of beauty 主张「帮助员工改变自\n我审美感知」；但原文 Since employers' perception of weight is what matters in\nthe labor market 明确表态「起作用的是雇主感知」，作者要改的是法律环境而不是员\n工自我感知。\nD) 与原文相反\nExcluding body shape as a category in the labor contract 写的是「把体型排除在劳\n动合同范畴之外」，与原文 include body type as a category 中 include（纳入）形成\nExcluding（排除）的反义对立，完全反向。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author suggest by saying \"The work-life balance is dead\"?", "options": ["A) The hope of achieving a thriving life is impossible to realize.", "B) The pursuit of a fulfilling career involves personal sacrifice.", "C) The imbalance between work and life simply doesn't exist anymore", "D) The concept of work-life balance contributes little to a fulfilling life."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （词义句意题）\n【判型】题干用 What does the author suggest by saying 的句式，直接询问作者说这句话想表达\n什么，是典型的句意理解题。\n【拆句】主干是 What does the author suggest，by 引导的方式状语 by saying \"The work-life\nbalance is dead\" 修饰 suggest，引号内是宾语从句的内容，整体平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The work-life balance is dead. By this, I'm not advocating that you should give up your\npursuit of having a fulfilling career and a thriving personal life, and I'm definitely not saying\nthat you have to give up one to have the other. I also acknowledge that we have a work-\nlife problem, but I'm arguing that the concept of balance has never been helpful, because it's\ntoo limiting.\n「工作-生活平衡」这个说法已经死了。我说这话不是劝你放弃追求充实的事业和蓬勃的私\n人生活，更不是说你必须放弃其一才能拥有另一个。我承认我们确实存在工作-生活问题，\n但我主张「平衡」这个概念从来都没什么用，因为它把人框得太死。\n【选项】A) 把对「概念」的否定偷换成对「目标」的否定\n选项说「实现充实人生的希望根本不可能实现」，而作者明确否认了这种解读：I'm not\nadvocating that you should give up your pursuit of having a fulfilling career and\na thriving personal life——他根本没说目标做不到，只说「平衡」这个词不好用。\nB) 无中生有\n选项说追求充实事业需要「个人牺牲」，文中通篇没讨论过 sacrifice 或牺牲——作者只\n是要换掉 balance 的提法，没说追求事业要牺牲生活。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项说工作和生活的失衡现象不再存在了，作者却紧接着承认 I also acknowledge that\nwe have a work-life problem——他承认问题真实存在，只是反对 balance 这个概\n念，与选项正相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe concept of balance has never been helpful ↔\nThe concept of work-life balance contributes little to a fulfilling life\na fulfilling career and a thriving personal life ↔ a fulfilling life\nThe concept of work-life balance contributes little to a fulfilling life 用「贡献甚\n微」换「从来没帮助」、用「充实人生」概括「事业加私生活」，正是定位句 the\nconcept of balance has never been helpful 的同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "词义句意题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author say about our use of language?", "options": ["A) It impacts how we think and behave.", "B) It changes with the passage of time.", "C) It reflects how we communicate.", "D) It differs from person to person."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say about our use of language 直接问作者关于某具体对象\n的明确表述，要求复述原文观点。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author say，about 介词短语 about our use of language 限定 say\n的内容范围，句式简单。\n【定位】You see, our language makes a difference, and how we refer to things matters because it\naffects our thinking and therefore our actions.\n你看，我们的语言会造成不同，我们如何称呼事物很重要，因为它影响我们的思维，进而影\n响我们的行动。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nit affects our thinking and therefore our actions ↔ It impacts how we think and behave\nIt impacts how we think and behave 同时覆盖「思维」和「行动」两个语义点，与定\n位句中的因果链完全对应，是定位句的最简同义改写。\nB) 无中生有\n选项说语言「随时间推移而变化」，原文谈的是语言对思维的影响，从未讨论语言本身\n的历时演变。\nC) 偷换概念\n选项说语言「反映」我们如何沟通，定位句说语言「影响」思维和行动——reflect（反\n映）和 affect（影响）方向相反：一个是被动镜像，一个是主动塑造。\nD) 无中生有\n选项说语言「因人而异」，原文谈的是语言对所有人的共同效应（影响思维与行动），\n完全没讨论个体差异。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author say we do in an ideal world?", "options": ["A) We do work that betters the lives of our families and friends.", "B) We do work that gives us bursts of joy each new day.", "C) We do meaningful work that contributes to society.", "D) We do demanding work that brings our capacity into full play."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say we do in an ideal world 限定在 ideal world 这一条件下\n询问具体做法，是单点细节复述。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author say we do，we do 是宾语从句省略 that，in an ideal world\n是地点状语；句式直白。\n【定位】In the ideal world, we're all doing work that we're proud of and that provides meaning and\npurpose to us. But even if your job doesn't give you shivers of joy each new day, working is a\npart of what each of us does and the contribution we make to society.\n在理想世界里，我们都在做让自己自豪、能为我们提供意义与目的的工作。但即便你的工作\n不会让你每天都因喜悦而颤抖，工作仍是我们每个人所做的事情之一，是我们对社会做出的\n贡献。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n选项说工作让「家人与朋友」的生活变得更好，原文说的是 the contribution we\nmake to society——贡献对象是「社会」，不是「家人与朋友」；且 support …\nour families, and the people around us 是讲「养活」，不是「理想世界中的工作\n内容」。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 even if your job doesn't give you shivers of joy each new day 用 even if 引导\n让步：即便工作不带来 shivers of joy，工作也仍是贡献——意味着作者承认「很多人的\n工作并不带来 shivers of joy」，选项却把「带来 shivers of joy」说成「理想世界的状\n态」，与原文意图相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nprovides meaning and purpose to us ↔ meaningful work\nthe contribution we make to society ↔ contributes to society\nWe do meaningful work that contributes to society 同时打中「有意义的工作」和\n「对社会有贡献」两个语义点，与定位句两层并列对应。\nD) 无中生有\n选项说「做让我们能力全开的高难度工作」，原文谈的是工作的「意义、目的、对社会\n贡献」，从未涉及工作难度或能力发挥维度。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say about life?", "options": ["A) It is cyclical.", "B) It is dynamic.", "C) It is fulfilling.", "D) It is risky."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about life 与第 52 题同款句式，问作者对 life 这个对象的明确\n陈述。\n【拆句】What does the author say about life 一句话主谓宾齐全，无从句、无否定，无陷阱。\n【定位】These variations are normal, and it's more useful to think of life as something that is ever\nevolving and changing, rather than a high-risk enterprise where things could go wrong with\none misstep.\n这些变化都是正常的，把人生看作不断演变、不断变化的事物，比把它看成「一步错就全盘\n崩」的高风险事业更有益。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n选项说生活是「循环往复」的，原文说的是「不断演变与变化」——演变是单向推进，\n循环是周期回到原点，两者方向不同。\nB) ✓ 正确\never evolving and changing ↔ It is dynamic\nThese variations are normal ↔ It is dynamic\nIt is dynamic 用「动态的」一词总括了原文 ever evolving、changing、variations 等\n同一语义场词汇，符合作者对 life 的定性。\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项说生活是「充实的」——但原文 provides meaning and purpose 修饰的是\nwork，不是 life；本题问的是作者对 life 的看法。\nD) 与原文相反\n选项说生活是「有风险的」，但原文 rather than a high-risk enterprise where things\ncould go wrong with one misstep 中的 rather than（而不是）正是要否定「高风\n险」这种看法——作者主张的恰恰是 life 不是 high-risk。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-09-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author advise us to do?", "options": ["A) Make life as simple as possible.", "B) Talk about balance in simpler terms.", "C) Balance life and work in a new way.", "D) Strive for a more fulfilling life."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题）\n【判型】What does the author advise us to do 不指向某段细节，而问作者贯穿全文给读者的最终\n主张，落在末段总结句，本质是主旨或态度类问题。\n【拆句】What does the author advise us to do，主干 the author advise us，to do 是不定式宾\n语补足语，结构平直。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n选项说让生活「尽可能简单」，原文从未谈过 simple 或 simplicity——作者主张的是\n「更宏大美好的 fulfillment」，与「简化」无关。\nB) 以偏概全\n选项把作者的呼吁缩到「用更简单的措辞谈论 balance」，但作者主张的是「抛弃\nbalance、转向 fulfillment」，不是「换种说法继续谈 balance」。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项说「用新方式平衡工作与生活」，但作者末段明确 Let's bury \"work-life\nbalance\"——要埋葬 balance，而不是用新方式继续 balance。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthink bigger and better about work-life fulfillment ↔ Strive for a more fulfilling life\na lot more living ↔ a more fulfilling life\nStrive for a more fulfilling life 用 strive for 对接末段 think bigger and better\nabout，用 a more fulfilling life 对接 work-life fulfillment 加 a lot more living——\n选项准确覆盖全文呼吁的两个核心词 fulfillment 与 living。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}]});
